86
00:04:10.713 --> 00:04:13.392
can we spend a few minutes talking about "Rabbit Hole?"

87
00:04:13.392 --> 00:04:14.790
Yeah, absolutely, whatever you want.

88
00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.240
So for people that aren't familiar,

89
00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.020
maybe it would be worth just providing

90
00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.913
a little bit of a synopsis.

91
00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.210
Sure, so "Rabbit Hole" is an eight part podcast

92
00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:30.520
that I helped produce and host with "The New York Times"

93
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.200
about basically the idea was to do a podcast

94
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.530
exploring sort of what the internet is doing to us.

95
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.180
So I'm a tech columnist, I report on the internet,

96
00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.030
and for a long time that meant reporting on gadgets,

97
00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.490
and companies that made gadgets,

98
00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.740
and software programs and new apps,

99
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.110
and social media platforms.

100
00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.041
And then a couple of years ago it really started to shift

101
00:04:51.041 --> 00:04:55.760
because what we saw is that a lot of the things

102
00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.770
that were happening in the offline world

103
00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.300
had sort of online antecedents,

104
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.550
had like things that were happening on the internet

105
00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.250
that led directly to things that happened

106
00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.083
in the offline world,

107
00:05:07.083 --> 00:05:10.000
and the most jarring and horrible example of that

108
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.224
was the shooting in Christchurch, New Zealand.

109
00:05:13.224 --> 00:05:17.418
And if people remember that was the really deadly one.

110
00:05:17.418 --> 00:05:19.806
I think 52 people ended up dying,

111
00:05:19.806 --> 00:05:22.470
and the shooter was a white nationalist

112
00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.040
who radicalized online,

113
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.850
and who was very obviously spending a lot of time online,

114
00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.376
and he live-streamed this shooting,

115
00:05:31.376 --> 00:05:34.090
and posted about it on the internet.

116
00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.149
And it was sort of like this internet event

117
00:05:37.149 --> 00:05:39.639
that was kind of engineered for virality.

118
00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:44.388
And it really like, I don't report on terrorism.

119
00:05:44.388 --> 00:05:46.660
I don't cover mass shootings,

120
00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.447
but this was such like an internet event.

121
00:05:49.447 --> 00:05:54.056
And so deeply tied to the kinds of extremism,

122
00:05:54.056 --> 00:05:57.078
and sort of violent rhetoric I'd seen

123
00:05:57.078 --> 00:05:59.679
on places like 4chan and 8chan

124
00:05:59.679 --> 00:06:02.820
that I just decided I had to look into that.

125
00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.840
And I had to figure out, like my question was basically,

126
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.660
I mean, I grew up on the internet.

127
00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.660
I'm like a child of the internet,

128
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.300
and I love technology and the internet.

129
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.980
And, also, I was seeing how it was radicalizing people,

130
00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.100
and turning them into extremists.

131
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:23.420
And so I wanted to figure out how that happened.

132
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.790
Like what is it actually doing to us?

133
00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.755
How do the systems and the algorithms interact

134
00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:32.840
with our information ecosystem?

135
00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.830
Who are the people who are sort of doing the radicalizing?

136
00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.483
Like what is this whole ecosystem,

137
00:06:37.483 --> 00:06:39.690
and how is it affecting who we are?

138
00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.205
So that's what we spent a year digging into

139
00:06:42.205 --> 00:06:44.484
starting with this guy, Caleb Cain,

140
00:06:44.484 --> 00:06:48.174
who was a 26-year-old kid from West Virginia,

141
00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:52.582
who got really into YouTube videos,

142
00:06:52.582 --> 00:06:56.634
and he became radicalized,

143
00:06:56.634 --> 00:07:00.380
and he was able to dig up and send us

144
00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.800
years worth of his YouTube browsing history.

145
00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.030
So we actually got to go back and kind of reconstruct

146
00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.440
how this guy who had supported Obama,

147
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.130
and cared about the environment,

148
00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:11.980
and loved Michael Moore movies,

149
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.200
morphed into this guy who was, like, deep in the kind of

150
00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.020
alt-right fever swamp,

151
00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.777
and actually trace his path sort of down the rabbit hole.

152
00:07:19.777 --> 00:07:23.600
And what was the decision to do it as a podcast,

153
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.910
as opposed to a long read,

154
00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:28.623
or a series of articles over a period of time?

155
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.480
Well, some of it did appear in articles,

156
00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.530
but it's really like, it's just different to hear it.

157
00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.950
I mean you know this from doing podcasts,

158
00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:41.010
like so much is conveyed in the voice,

159
00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.055
and so much is conveyed in hearing things

160
00:07:44.055 --> 00:07:46.130
rather than reading them and so,

161
00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:47.460
especially, with this series,

162
00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.267
which was all about sort of internet media,

163
00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:53.440
and the charismatic people on YouTube

164
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.280
who are radicalizing young white men,

165
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.370
like, it's just much more compelling

166
00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:59.010
to hear those people talk

167
00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.658
than to read transcriptions of what they're saying.

168
00:08:02.658 --> 00:08:06.408
And, ultimately, like, we had an amazing team of people

169
00:08:06.408 --> 00:08:11.408
putting this together and they sort of came up with the idea

170
00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:15.780
of making the whole show sort of sound like the experience

171
00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.470
of falling into an internet rabbit hole.

172
00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:18.303
Right.

173
00:08:18.303 --> 00:08:19.630
So if you pay attention, it's sort of like,

174
00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:22.398
it's kind of engineered in this really sort of careful,

175
00:08:22.398 --> 00:08:24.010
and thoughtful, and cool way

176
00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:29.010
where there are no, like, intros and outros of the episodes.

177
00:08:29.098 --> 00:08:32.410
It just goes, like, straight into one episode from another.

178
00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.551
And it sort of, like, mimics the feeling

179
00:08:34.551 --> 00:08:37.039
of falling into an internet rabbit hole.

180
00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.170
I'm sure everyone's done this,

181
00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:41.410
you start watching one YouTube video,

182
00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:42.791
and then four hours later you're like,

183
00:08:42.791 --> 00:08:46.387
why am I watching, like, videos of trains in Norway?

184
00:08:46.387 --> 00:08:47.929
Right, right, right.

185
00:08:47.929 --> 00:08:49.900
Like, how did I get here?

186
00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:50.910
And that's sort of the feeling

187
00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:52.390
that we wanted to recreate in the show.

188
00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.530
Yeah, well, you were very successful in that regard.

189
00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.450
I mean, it is truly a sonic experience,

190
00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:58.550
and it was fascinating

191
00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:02.180
to go on this archeological exploration

192
00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.370
of how this young man step-by-step

193
00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.630
finds his way from one place to a very different place,

194
00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.080
which then, of course,

195
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.130
brings up the subject of the algorithm,

196
00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:14.930
the nature of the algorithm,

197
00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.170
and how this is being managed by YouTube.

198
00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:21.613
You have a couple interviews with Susan Wojcicki at YouTube.

199
00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.270
And I guess my question is

200
00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.737
so much has happened since "Rabbit Hole."

201
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.590
What is your sense of what that algorithm looks like now,

202
00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.830
or the job that YouTube is doing

203
00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.210
to protect against these sorts of

204
00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:42.110
acts of violence in the future?

205
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:42.994
Well, I think YouTube

206
00:09:42.994 --> 00:09:45.520
is doing a better job than they were.

207
00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.201
I mean, when we started reporting this series

208
00:09:48.201 --> 00:09:51.943
they were still sort of saying there wasn't a problem,

209
00:09:51.943 --> 00:09:55.240
that they didn't have extremists on their service.

210
00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.440
It was they were kind of just like bizarrely uninterested

211
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.510
in dealing with what I saw, and a lot of people saw

212
00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.740
as sort of this growing threat of radicalization

213
00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.531
that was happening on YouTube primarily,

214
00:10:07.531 --> 00:10:09.290
and not just on YouTube,

215
00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.069
but through YouTube's recommendation algorithm,

216
00:10:12.069 --> 00:10:16.180
which is responsible for something like 70%

217
00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.020
of all the time people spend on YouTube

218
00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.300
is due to that little sidebar,

219
00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.690
that's powered by some of the most sophisticated AI

220
00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:24.463
that's ever been created.

221
00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:28.360
And so that's the piece that we wanted to sort of connect

222
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.610
was how is the algorithm sort of pulling people into,

223
00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:33.573
it's not just the people are falling into rabbit holes,

224
00:10:33.573 --> 00:10:35.529
it's that they're sort of being pulled,

225
00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:39.760
and what is that force, and how has that evolved over time?

226
00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.249
And so I think they've made a lot of changes

227
00:10:41.249 --> 00:10:43.870
since the series came out.

228
00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:46.498
They've updated their policies on hate speech,

229
00:10:46.498 --> 00:10:47.969
and white nationalism.

230
00:10:47.969 --> 00:10:50.510
They're kicking neo-Nazis off.

231
00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:54.590
Some of the worst people in that world

232
00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.019
have been sort of de-platformed,

233
00:10:56.019 --> 00:10:59.023
and they've changed their recommendation algorithm now.

234
00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:04.132
They call it borderline content, like stuff that basically

235
00:11:04.132 --> 00:11:06.390
doesn't violate their rules technically,

236
00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.188
but is like not great.

237
00:11:08.188 --> 00:11:12.080
And they're showing something like 70% fewer recommendations

238
00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:14.710
for that kind of content through this algorithm now.

239
00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.100
So I think there's still obviously more to do.

240
00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:21.500
There's still a ton of extremism on every internet platform,

241
00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.710
and, YouTube, I think has a ways to go in that regard,

242
00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:27.890
but I think they're at least aware of the problem now

243
00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.040
in a way that they maybe weren't when we started reporting.

244
00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:33.580
Yeah, and in fairness, and you pose this question, too.

245
00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.670
His name is name is Caleb, right?

246
00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:35.503
The young man.

247
00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.493
Yep.

248
00:11:37.493 --> 00:11:40.860
The very same algorithm that prompted him

249
00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:44.610
to descend down this rabbit hole is the algorithm

250
00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:49.380
that also ultimately introduces him to other ideas,

251
00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.770
and helps him start to critically think about

252
00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:57.650
his long-held beliefs and ultimately change them.

253
00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.650
So the argument could be made that the algorithm ultimately

254
00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:04.870
served a positive purpose,

255
00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:07.610
or worked in a way that is counterintuitive

256
00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:10.160
to the less nuanced narrative

257
00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.893
that it just pushes people in one direction only.

258
00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:17.320
For sure, I mean, this algorithm,

259
00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.638
the thing is it has no idea what it's recommended, right?

260
00:12:20.638 --> 00:12:22.800
