that I don't think they anticipated.

520
00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:31.964
I talked to one guy who was a former YouTube engineer,

521
00:24:31.964 --> 00:24:34.130
Guillaume Chaslot,

522
00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:36.720
who was part of the team that developed the AI.

523
00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.540
And he sort of brought up some of these concerns.

524
00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.473
He said, I think we're only showing people

525
00:24:41.473 --> 00:24:42.605
one side of the story.

526
00:24:42.605 --> 00:24:45.484
We're feeding them conspiracy theories.

527
00:24:45.484 --> 00:24:49.193
And he was kind of ignored and eventually got pushed out.

528
00:24:49.193 --> 00:24:53.191
And so I think the goals that we give our algorithms

529
00:24:53.191 --> 00:24:54.763
are so, so important,

530
00:24:54.763 --> 00:24:56.980
and it doesn't have to be watch time.

531
00:24:56.980 --> 00:24:58.190
It doesn't have to be clicks.

532
00:24:58.190 --> 00:24:59.449
It doesn't have to be outrage.

533
00:24:59.449 --> 00:25:01.850
It doesn't have to be engagement.

534
00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.330
It could be something else.

535
00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.899
And I think the tech companies need to adjust

536
00:25:05.899 --> 00:25:07.090
what they're aiming for.

537
00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:08.740
And I think good things could come of that.

538
00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:11.343
Hmm, so that's an optimistic bent.

539
00:25:11.343 --> 00:25:15.160
I'm mostly optimistic. I mean, I know I write about

540
00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:16.765
horrible things happening on the internet,

541
00:25:16.765 --> 00:25:19.700
but, actually, like, this surprises people

542
00:25:19.700 --> 00:25:22.573
because my, like, Twitter feed is all, like, QAnon,

543
00:25:22.573 --> 00:25:25.580
and like extremists and neo-Nazis.

544
00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:30.093
And like I deal in like a lot of like really gross stuff

545
00:25:30.093 --> 00:25:31.472
happening on the internet,

546
00:25:31.472 --> 00:25:35.083
but I am actually, like, I'm optimistic about people.

547
00:25:35.083 --> 00:25:38.061
I'm not optimistic about, like, all people,

548
00:25:38.061 --> 00:25:40.580
but I'm optimistic about

549
00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:41.942
that if you give people good information

550
00:25:41.942 --> 00:25:43.940
they will make better choices.

551
00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.380
And I think that one thing

552
00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:50.028
that we sort of often don't realize

553
00:25:50.028 --> 00:25:53.221
is that algorithms are just people.

554
00:25:53.221 --> 00:25:57.534
I mean, algorithms are not some magical detached thing.

555
00:25:57.534 --> 00:26:00.290
They're built by humans who give them goals,

556
00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:01.810
who tell them what to maximize,

557
00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:06.251
who calibrate them, and train the models.

558
00:26:06.251 --> 00:26:09.280
Cathy O'Neil, who's a great writer on the subject

559
00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:12.253
says that algorithms are opinions embedded in code.

560
00:26:12.253 --> 00:26:16.300
And I think when we start to realize that these algorithms

561
00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.620
aren't some iron law of the universe,

562
00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:20.261
that they can be changed and adjusted,

563
00:26:20.261 --> 00:26:21.790
it becomes a lot more hopeful

564
00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:24.171
because you start to realize, like, how it is right now

565
00:26:24.171 --> 00:26:25.790
isn't how it has to be.

566
00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:27.430
Sure, but at the same time,

567
00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:30.510
there is a fundamental misalignment of incentives

568
00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:34.890
across all of these big

569
00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.140
social media platforms

570
00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:40.660
in that when they're oriented around maximizing watch time,

571
00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:42.460
or keeping you on the platform,

572
00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:44.850
it's generally for the purpose of serving you up

573
00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:46.410
as many ads as possible

574
00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:49.199
so that they can ratchet up their ad revenue,

575
00:26:49.199 --> 00:26:53.450
and until or unless there is a new sort of

576
00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:57.630
conceptual orientation around what a social media platform

577
00:26:57.630 --> 00:26:58.680
can and should be

578
00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:03.090
where the incentives are set up in a healthier dynamic,

579
00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:08.786
we're gonna continue to see the kind of bad actors,

580
00:27:08.786 --> 00:27:13.168
and gaming of the system that we've seen today.

581
00:27:13.168 --> 00:27:15.647
Totally, and that's why I think we need new incentives

582
00:27:15.647 --> 00:27:17.950
for these networks,

583
00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:20.020
and for the networks that are going to replace them.

584
00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:21.800
I mean, every social network

585
00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:22.950
gets replaced eventually, right?

586
00:27:22.950 --> 00:27:25.690
It's sort of one of the parts of,

587
00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:29.040
one of the most predictable things in the tech world

588
00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:31.450
is that every company dies and is replaced.

589
00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:34.250
Every MySpace has a Facebook.

590
00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:36.968
Every Friendster has a MySpace.

591
00:27:36.968 --> 00:27:40.420
And so I think what we're trying to figure out right now

592
00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:41.830
is like what's gonna come after

593
00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:43.060
our current social platforms,

594
00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:44.100
and how will they be different?

595
00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:47.511
And my hope is that seeing how the last generation

596
00:27:47.511 --> 00:27:49.690
sort of screwed things up,

597
00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:52.818
and how long it took them to fix their problems

598
00:27:52.818 --> 00:27:55.980
will instruct the people building the next generation,

599
00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:58.070
and maybe they'll still make mistakes.

600
00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.170
And this is still very much a work in progress.

601
00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:04.100
And I think some of this is just sort of us evolving,

602
00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.890
or adapting to our new environment technologically,

603
00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:11.410
but I'm hopeful when I see people

604
00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:13.588
making more thoughtful decisions at these companies,

605
00:28:13.588 --> 00:28:17.467
not that necessarily those companies will become nonprofits,

606
00:28:17.467 --> 00:28:19.260
and give back all the money that they made

607
00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:20.910
from promoting extremism, or whatever,

608
00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:23.420
but just that people are at least aware

609
00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:24.480
that these pitfalls are out there.

610
00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:27.650
Yeah, sure, I mean, I suppose the current test case

611
00:28:27.650 --> 00:28:30.160
in the Petri dish right now is Clubhouse,

612
00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:31.640
and this is something you've reported on,

613
00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:33.950
and made a very keen observation,

614
00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:37.650
which is that all of the things that we traditionally see

615
00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:39.740
in the arc of a social media platform

616
00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:42.810
are all kind of happening simultaneously with Clubhouse.

617
00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:45.990
Like it starts out like this utopian place

618
00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.770
where everybody's sharing ideas and it's wonderful,

619
00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:52.740
and then enter the bad actors, and then, okay,

620
00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:54.900
so what are the community guidelines gonna be?

621
00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:56.420
And then how are we gonna monetize that?

622
00:28:56.420 --> 00:29:00.640
But this is all occurring with a very compressed timeline,

623
00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:05.410
which I think is interesting in that it elucidates,

624
00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.890
like how fast everything is moving now,

625
00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:09.760
like the fact that this life cycle

626
00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:12.310
is occurring instantaneously

627
00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:15.360
when with Facebook it took years and years and years

628
00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:19.600
for this kind of dialogue and progression to unfold.

629
00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:22.650
Totally, this is all happening so fast.

630
00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:26.209
And, like, Clubhouse isn't even technically released yet.

631
00:29:26.209 --> 00:29:27.385
I know, it's still like in beta.

632
00:29:27.385 --> 00:29:29.067
It's still in beta, right?

633
00:29:29.067 --> 00:29:33.108
But it's already been through like the first 10 years

634
00:29:33.108 --> 00:29:35.377
of Facebook's problems.

635
00:29:35.377 --> 00:29:38.240
And it's now sort of trying to figure out,

636
00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:39.259
like, where to draw the lines,

637
00:29:39.259 --> 00:29:42.336
but I think Clubhouse is really instructive to me because,

638
00:29:42.336 --> 00:29:43.620
I don't know how much time you spend on there,

639
00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.680
but I spend a fair bit of time on there.

640
00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:49.328
And it seems to me like therapy for people who have been,

641
00:29:49.328 --> 00:29:53.370
like, traumatized by other social media platforms.

642
00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:56.700
It's like group therapy where you're like,

643
00:29:56.700 --> 00:29:58.335
okay, what did we just do to ourselves

644
00:29:58.335 --> 00:29:59.848
for the last 10 years?

645
00:29:59.848 --> 00:30:02.440
Like I was in a room the other day,

646
00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:04.246
and the topic was sort of like,

647
00:30:04.246 --> 00:30:08.446
why we hate our Twitter personas.

648
00:30:08.446 --> 00:30:11.710
Like a lot of people I think have spent years

649
00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:14.454
crafting these personas that aren't even really them,

650
00:30:14.454 --> 00:30:16.660
and that they sort of grow to hate

651
00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:18.730
because everything has to be a little sharper

652
00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:19.670
for the retweets.

653
00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:22.313
And like you've got to, like, throw throw the jabs in there,

654
00:30:22.313 --> 00:30:23.639
and, like, start fights.

655
00:30:23.639 --> 00:30:25.743
And, like, maybe you're saying things

656
00:30:25.743 --> 00:30:27.280
that you don't even really mean.

657
00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:28.300
And you look at them later,

658
00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:29.960
and you're like, why did I do that?

659
00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:32.269
And I think people are starting to really,

660
00:30:32.269 --> 00:30:34.496
there's like this growing feeling of like,

661
00:30:34.496 --> 00:30:38.528
I don't like who I show up as on the internet,

662
00:30:38.528 --> 00:30:40.150
on social media.

663
00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:43.664
And so Clubhouse to me feels like a response to that piece

664
00:30:43.664 --> 00:30:45.330
of where we all are right now.

665
00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:46.240
Yeah, that's cool.

666
00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:48.100
I mean, I've been a bit of a lurker there.

667
00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:50.260
I have yet to chime in on anything.

668
00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:52.600
I'm gonna host my first Clubhouse chat

669
00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:53.577
in another week or two,

670
00:30:53.577 --> 00:30:57.357
but I've just been trying to sort of listen in

671
00:30:57.357 --> 00:31:00.050
like yourself, and figure out what's going on.

672
00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:03.030
And it does seem to me fundamentally different.

673
00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:06.540
There is something about the participatory aspect of it.

674
00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:08.430
It's like being on a conference call

675
00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:09.860
with a whole bunch of people

676
00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:13.510
where everybody gets to say the fact that it's not recorded.

677
00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:17.967
And then it disappears afterwards feels like it sort of,

678
00:31:17.967 --> 00:31:21.476
it's like a pressure valve release on that idea

679
00:31:21.476 --> 00:31:24.968
that everything I say has to be finely honed,

680
00:31:24.968 --> 00:31:27.340
and directly on point.

681
00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:30.770
And it seems to be supportive in general.

682
00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:32.396
Like everybody conducts themselves

683
00:31:32.396 --> 00:31:34.346
respectfully of each other.

684
00:31:34.346 --> 00:31:37.070
Maybe there's rooms where that's not occurring,

685
00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:39.230
but that's been my experience to date.

686
00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:41.579
Yeah, I mean, to me it feels more human.

687
00:31:41.579 --> 00:31:45.796
It feels like we are more complicated people on Clubhouse

688
00:31:45.796 --> 00:31:47.220
than we are on Twitter

689
00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:49.976
where we're all sort of flattened down into these like,

690
00:31:49.976 --> 00:31:52.320
two-dimensional avatars

691
00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:54.543
that just, like, fight with each other all the time.

692
00:31:54.543 --> 00:31:57.120
I've had people who like,

693
00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:59.425
call me the worst names possible on Twitter,

694
00:31:59.425 --> 00:32:01.600
and then they show up in a Clubhouse room,

695
00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:05.130
and they're perfectly pleasant and cordial.

696
00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:07.520
And it's like, what happened to that person?

697
00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:09.740
Like, yeah, like where did that guy go,

698
00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:12.593
and why is he not calling me a communist?

699
00:32:14.086 --> 00:32:17.270
It feels like and I don't know,

700
00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:18.670
I mean, Clubhouse has problems,

701
00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.020
like there's tons of harassment and hate speeches.

702
00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:22.040
It's got all the problems

703
00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:25.010
that any big social network would have,

704
00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:27.540
but I do think we're starting to see new models emerging

705
00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:30.287
that maybe allow people to be a little bit more human.

706
00:32:30.287 --> 00:32:33.730
I mean, having a conversation voice to voice

707
00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:36.674
is so much different than having it avatar to avatar.

708
00:32:36.674 --> 00:32:40.270
And I think it's better and think it's more humane,

709
00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.760
and I think it leads to better places.

710
00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.790
And so I think that's what the next generation

711
00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:47.300
of tech platforms are gonna be built around

712
00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:50.643
is, like, how do we show up as ourselves on the internet,

713
00:32:50.643 --> 00:32:52.884
and get away from these kind of

714
00:32:52.884 --> 00:32:54.706
two-dimensional caricatures we've created?

715
00:32:54.706 --> 00:32:59.160
Sure, what's your sense of how it will impact podcasting?

716
00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:00.590
I don't think it's the same thing.

717
00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:02.997
I mean, there's some overlap,

718
00:33:02.997 --> 00:33:07.520
especially the kind of, like, more produced Clubhouse rooms

719
00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:10.790
where you have hosts and guests, and some parts

720
00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:13.797
there's sort of like a little Venn diagram middle there,

721
00:33:13.797 --> 00:33:15.687
but it's so different,

722
00:33:15.687 --> 00:33:18.672
and podcasts are wonderful

723
00:33:18.672 --> 00:33:21.187
because you can really go deep on a subject.

724
00:33:21.187 --> 00:33:24.820
Clubhouse, I found is sort of more like,

725
00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:29.216
it's like more like going to a bar, and you're talking,

726
00:33:29.216 --> 00:33:33.744
and one minute you're talking about video games,

727
00:33:33.744 --> 00:33:35.800
and the next minute you're talking about crypto,

728
00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:39.911
and it's just sort of this sort of moving conversation

729
00:33:39.911 --> 00:33:41.815
that is kind of fun to be part of.

730
00:33:41.815 --> 00:33:44.300
My big thing about Clubhouse is I don't know

731
00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:46.126
whether it's gonna outlast the pandemic.

732
00:33:46.126 --> 00:33:49.035
I think that when people can go outside again,

733
00:33:49.035 --> 00:33:51.180
and talk to their friends and go to bars,

734
00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:54.690
like, I can't see myself spending

735
00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:56.060
three hours a night on my phone

736
00:33:56.060 --> 00:34:01.060
listening to some guy talk about NFTs for artists.

737
00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:04.220
Well, I think there will be some breakout Clubhouse stars,

738
00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:05.520
and those are gonna be the people

739
00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:08.250
who are showing up consistently to host

740
00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:11.120
a very kind of curated experience.

741
00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:14.275
So it's gonna be less about the meandering nonsense,

742
00:34:14.275 --> 00:34:16.830
and I think more targeted.

743
00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.890
And with that you'll see monetization

744
00:34:19.890 --> 00:34:23.680
whether it's subscriptions, or ad supported shows.

745
00:34:23.680 --> 00:34:26.800
So I think it will not only survive but thrive,

746
00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:29.280
but I think the early entries

747
00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:32.715
where it's just a bunch of people talking about nothing,

748
00:34:32.715 --> 00:34:35.696
I do agree, I think that people will tire of that.

749
00:34:35.696 --> 00:34:38.930
I mean, that was a lot of podcasts, too,

750
00:34:38.930 --> 00:34:40.823
in the early days, right?
It's a lot of podcasts now.

751
00:34:40.823 --> 00:34:41.810
It's just, like, people talking about nothing.

752
00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:45.137
It still is, there are like two million podcasts,

753
00:34:45.137 --> 00:34:46.559
so there's plenty of that.

754
00:34:46.559 --> 00:34:50.780
Right, so I think as platforms mature,

755
00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:52.219
like the bar kind of gets higher,

756
00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:54.230
like you need to show up

757
00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:57.719
with a little bit more fire to, like, make a dent,

758
00:34:57.719 --> 00:34:59.720
but I think right now,

759
00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:01.590
Clubhouse is still in the weird experimental phase,

760
00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:03.770
which I love.

761
00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:06.470
Like I have no interest in, like, the boring,

762
00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:08.420
like, business Clubhouse rooms.

763
00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:10.130
I love the weirdos.

764
00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:11.952
Like there's this group the other day

765
00:35:11.952 --> 00:35:15.400
it was called, like, Whale Moan.

766
00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:18.070
And it was all people just moaning like whales

767
00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:19.433
in a Clubhouse room.

768
00:35:20.888 --> 00:35:22.070
Uh-huh.

769
00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:25.797
There's like this group called the Cotton Club

770
00:35:25.797 --> 00:35:28.870
where, like, you go in and you change your avatar

771
00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:30.130
to a black and white photo,

772
00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:32.010
and you pretend like you're in the 1920s.

773
00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:34.110
And someone says, like, hey, Rich,

774
00:35:34.110 --> 00:35:35.290
like, what are you having tonight?

775
00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:36.123
What are you drinking?

776
00:35:36.123 --> 00:35:37.490
And there's jazz playing,

777
00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:40.623
It's like sort of a fun, like, virtual world.

778
00:35:40.623 --> 00:35:42.180
So I love the weirdos

779
00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:43.290
who are experimenting with stuff on there.

780
00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:45.853
Yeah, cool, all right, well, one more thing about

781
00:35:45.853 --> 00:35:49.110
"Rabbit Hole" before we move on.

