811
00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:15.027
And when I started hearing about QAnon

812
00:37:15.027 --> 00:37:17.825
a couple of years ago, it really triggered the, like,

813
00:37:17.825 --> 00:37:20.046
oh, this is a religion thing for me.

814
00:37:20.046 --> 00:37:21.805
It was, like, there are all these people,

815
00:37:21.805 --> 00:37:24.580
they believe in this, like, narrative about

816
00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:27.740
how the world operates, and whose pulling the strings,

817
00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:30.830
and, like, these powers beyond our comprehension.

818
00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:34.060
And they're following this mystical character on 4chan,

819
00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:35.700
who's dropping little clues

820
00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:37.810
that they're all sort of interpreting together,

821
00:37:37.810 --> 00:37:39.970
and it's filling that sort of community need

822
00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:41.223
in a lot of their lives.

823
00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:45.410
People think QAnon is just like a bunch of wackos,

824
00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:47.540
like, being insane on the internet.

825
00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:48.570
And there is some of that,

826
00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:50.670
but people are also using this as

827
00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:52.483
it's become people's social lives.

828
00:37:52.483 --> 00:37:54.102
They're making friends.

829
00:37:54.102 --> 00:37:57.293
It's an activity that they do together on the internet.

830
00:37:57.293 --> 00:37:59.600
It's a lot of fun for them.

831
00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:02.420
And so I felt a real parallel between that,

832
00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:03.750
and sort of the religious reporting

833
00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:05.960
that I had done earlier in my career.

834
00:38:05.960 --> 00:38:09.220
And so I just decided, like, I got to investigate this.

835
00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:11.620
So I spent a long time talking to QAnon believers,

836
00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.897
hanging out in chat rooms listening to their conversations,

837
00:38:14.897 --> 00:38:17.390
reading up on the movement.

838
00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:20.029
And we did these two episodes about QAnon.

839
00:38:20.029 --> 00:38:23.890
And then after that, like, at the time QAnon

840
00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:25.700
was sort of like this novelty thing.

841
00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:28.750
It was like, oh, look at these interesting weirdos.

842
00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:31.007
Like they may be a little violent sometimes

843
00:38:31.007 --> 00:38:33.568
around the edges, but like mostly,

844
00:38:33.568 --> 00:38:35.900
it's just strange and troubling,

845
00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:37.755
but, like, maybe not an immediate threat.

846
00:38:37.755 --> 00:38:40.204
And then they marched on the Capitol, right?

847
00:38:40.204 --> 00:38:41.037
Right.

848
00:38:41.037 --> 00:38:44.230
And it was, like, the QAnon riot at the Capitol.

849
00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:49.174
And QAnon was a big part of the Stop the Steal thing.

850
00:38:49.174 --> 00:38:51.500
And suddenly it was, like,

851
00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:55.790
oh, this is not just like a cult of internet weirdos.

852
00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:58.080
This is like an extremist political movement

853
00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:02.786
that is like creating real offline violence and harms.

854
00:39:02.786 --> 00:39:05.590
So yeah, I mean, I think there's so much more there.

855
00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:07.100
I'm still fascinated by QAnon.

856
00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:09.326
I think the movement is kind of dissipating

857
00:39:09.326 --> 00:39:13.070
because Trump isn't the president anymore,

858
00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:14.730
which was the whole fun of it

859
00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:16.147
was that Trump was the president.

860
00:39:16.147 --> 00:39:19.320
And so now I think a lot of their predictions

861
00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:20.400
have not come true.

862
00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.410
There are no mass arrests

863
00:39:22.410 --> 00:39:24.770
of satanic pedophiles in government.

864
00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:26.330
And so I think a lot of people have sort of

865
00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:27.630
quietly moved on.

866
00:39:27.630 --> 00:39:29.214
And as far as season two,

867
00:39:29.214 --> 00:39:33.190
you should talk to my my bosses at "The New York Times,"

868
00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:36.870
but it's really, like, I think it would shock you

869
00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:38.490
how hard it is to make something like that.

870
00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:40.090
Oh, you don't have to tell me.

871
00:39:40.090 --> 00:39:40.923
Like.

872
00:39:40.923 --> 00:39:44.199
I mean, I can't imagine, like I have such an appreciation

873
00:39:44.199 --> 00:39:47.900
for the production quality of what you guys created.

874
00:39:47.900 --> 00:39:51.810
I mean, that is a Herculean effort to pull that together,

875
00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:54.880
and to kind of create the experience that you created.

876
00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:56.650
I know how much work it is just for me

877
00:39:56.650 --> 00:39:59.110
to do simple conversations with people.

878
00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:02.240
To do what you do is an entirely different thing.

879
00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:05.690
Like I understand that requires a tremendous amount of time,

880
00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:07.680
and resources and money, et cetera,

881
00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:10.960
but it's tremendously valuable.

882
00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:12.517
Thank you, yeah.

883
00:40:12.517 --> 00:40:14.273
I'm happy to call your bosses.

884
00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:16.693
Yeah, please do.

885
00:40:16.693 --> 00:40:18.210
I mean, I was shocked by that, too,

886
00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:19.974
'cause I come from the world of like writing,

887
00:40:19.974 --> 00:40:22.140
which is like time-consuming,

888
00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:23.720
and requires a lot of thoughtfulness,

889
00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:27.903
but I think podcasting that kind of podcasting,

890
00:40:27.903 --> 00:40:30.412
the kind of hyper produced narrative podcast

891
00:40:30.412 --> 00:40:32.167
is actually more like making a film

892
00:40:32.167 --> 00:40:34.414
than writing a newspaper article.

893
00:40:34.414 --> 00:40:36.110
And I didn't appreciate that.

894
00:40:36.110 --> 00:40:38.264
Like you gather hundreds of hours of tape,

895
00:40:38.264 --> 00:40:41.446
and then you cut it down into 36 minutes.

896
00:40:41.446 --> 00:40:43.920
And it's like all this work,

897
00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:45.340
and then you're, like, that's it, eight episodes?

898
00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:47.405
Right, 27 minutes, yeah.

899
00:40:47.405 --> 00:40:50.730
Right, right, but I think it's,

900
00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:54.130
I mean, it was a ton of fun, very rewarding.

901
00:40:54.130 --> 00:40:56.220
I love podcasts as a medium,

902
00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:58.250
and I hope I can keep doing them.

903
00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:01.130
So yeah, if want to call up my bosses over that,

904
00:41:01.130 --> 00:41:02.723
I would not turn you down.

905
00:41:02.723 --> 00:41:04.954
Cool, well, let's talk about the new book.

906
00:41:04.954 --> 00:41:09.612
First of all, it's pub day for you, so congrats on that.

907
00:41:09.612 --> 00:41:10.920
That's very exciting.

908
00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:11.753
Thank you.

909
00:41:11.753 --> 00:41:14.366
It's not that much of a leap

910
00:41:14.366 --> 00:41:19.366
given where your focus has been talking about algorithms,

911
00:41:19.806 --> 00:41:24.316
which is by its very nature a discussion around automation

912
00:41:24.316 --> 00:41:28.365
to the broader conversation around AI,

913
00:41:28.365 --> 00:41:33.053
and what the future and the present looks like

914
00:41:33.053 --> 00:41:37.366
in terms of our personal lives, our careers,

915
00:41:37.366 --> 00:41:39.606
and what our communities look like.

916
00:41:39.606 --> 00:41:42.596
And what I appreciate about the book

917
00:41:42.596 --> 00:41:44.212
is that you approached it,

918
00:41:44.212 --> 00:41:48.500
well, first of all it's like part primer on what AI is,

919
00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:51.678
and what the nature of automation truly looks like,

920
00:41:51.678 --> 00:41:56.678
but then it's also this sort of a prescriptive manual

921
00:41:57.355 --> 00:41:59.630
on how to deal with all of this.

922
00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:02.147
It's like, it is in many ways a self-help book,

923
00:42:02.147 --> 00:42:06.730
which is very cool, but it's laced with nuance.

924
00:42:06.730 --> 00:42:09.330
It's neither utopian nor dystopian.

925
00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:11.950
Like you're really trying to grapple with all of this

926
00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:13.515
in the most objective way.

927
00:42:13.515 --> 00:42:17.830
So maybe we could start with just what prompted you

928
00:42:17.830 --> 00:42:19.190
to want to write a book about this?

929
00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:21.408
Like what was the question lingering in your mind

930
00:42:21.408 --> 00:42:23.376
that led to this work?

931
00:42:23.376 --> 00:42:27.300
Well, it was literally a self-help book.

932
00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:29.020
I mean, I was mostly asking,

933
00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:31.110
like, is this gonna happen to me?

934
00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:34.171
Like am I gonna lose my job to some algorithm?

935
00:42:34.171 --> 00:42:37.250
Am I turning into a zombie who just,

936
00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:39.430
like, scrolls through his feeds all day,

937
00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:41.510
and then goes to bed and wakes up and does it again,

938
00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:44.900
like, I was really worried about what I was becoming

939
00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:48.367
as a result of these algorithms, and this automation,

940
00:42:48.367 --> 00:42:51.920
and this AI and what my future was gonna look like, so.

941
00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:55.050
I go to a lot of tech events for work,

942
00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:57.350
and I talk to a lot of people in the industry.

943
00:42:58.250 --> 00:42:59.471
This was a few years ago,

944
00:42:59.471 --> 00:43:02.765
and I was really sort of shocked by how simplistic,

945
00:43:02.765 --> 00:43:06.980
and sort of theoretical the conversation around AI,

946
00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:07.969
and automation was.

947
00:43:07.969 --> 00:43:11.098
There were people who said all of this is terrible.

948
00:43:11.098 --> 00:43:13.230
Robots are gonna take all the jobs.

949
00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:14.410
We're gonna be unemployed,

950
00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:16.050
and homeless, and it's gonna be awful.

951
00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:18.286
We're gonna be the people in "Wall-E"

952
00:43:18.286 --> 00:43:22.068
driving around on our chairs drinking Big Gulps.

953
00:43:22.068 --> 00:43:24.870
And there are other people who think

954
00:43:24.870 --> 00:43:25.890
this is gonna be amazing,

955
00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:28.520
and AI is gonna help us discover new cancer drugs,

956
00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:30.593
and fix the climate crisis.

957
00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:34.490
And those were sort of binary opinions,

958
00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:35.739
but there wasn't a lot in the middle.

959
00:43:35.739 --> 00:43:39.180
And there also wasn't much that felt practical.

960
00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:43.530
Like what do I actually do if I am working in a job

961
00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:45.120
that is likely to be automated?

962
00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:47.889
How do I know if I'm in the risk zone?

963
00:43:47.889 --> 00:43:51.590
And if I am like what can I do?

964
00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:52.810
Do I go back to school?

965
00:43:52.810 --> 00:43:53.950
Do I learn to code?

966
00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:56.490
Like, do I start sucking up to Alexa

967
00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:59.080
so that when the robot revolution comes

968
00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:01.880
I end up on the robot's side,

969
00:44:01.880 --> 00:44:05.160
like what is the actual action item here?

970
00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:06.960
And so I couldn't find that,

971
00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:08.970
like, I kept just trying to find,

972
00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:11.230
like, anything that would help me deal with this problem,

973
00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:12.200
and it didn't exist.

974
00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:14.860
And so I decided to write it.

975
00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:18.670
So the first half of the book is the kind of diagnosis part.

976
00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.890
That's like what is actually happening with AI,

977
00:44:20.890 --> 00:44:23.640
and automation, how is it changing the world around us?

978
00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:25.750
It's not about the future it's about the present,

979
00:44:25.750 --> 00:44:28.345
and the ways that these things are showing up in our lives,

980
00:44:28.345 --> 00:44:29.810
and what it's doing to us.

981
00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:31.440
And then the second half, as you said,

982
00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:34.050
is the more prescriptive part where I'm telling people,

983
00:44:34.050 --> 00:44:35.759
like, here are nine things you can do

984
00:44:35.759 --> 00:44:40.368
to futureproof your life, your career, and your community.

985
00:44:40.368 --> 00:44:42.440
So let's talk a little bit about

986
00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:44.420
what's happening in the present,

987
00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:47.477
because I think it sort of belies this conventional notion

988
00:44:47.477 --> 00:44:51.399
that AI or automation is all about,

989
00:44:51.399 --> 00:44:53.524
robots on the factory floor.

990
00:44:53.524 --> 00:44:56.115
And that's certainly the case,

991
00:44:56.115 --> 00:44:58.140
but that's almost like the past,

992
00:44:58.140 --> 00:44:59.490
like that's been accomplished.

993
00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:03.200
And there is a lot of AI happening right now,

994
00:45:03.200 --> 00:45:06.028
right underneath our feet that we're unaware of,

995
00:45:06.028 --> 00:45:10.290
but has real kind of existential implications

996
00:45:10.290 --> 00:45:13.588
in terms of the workforce not just with blue collar workers,

997
00:45:13.588 --> 00:45:17.386
but now very much white collar workers.

998
00:45:17.386 --> 00:45:21.200
Yeah, I think we still have this outdated notion

999
00:45:21.200 --> 00:45:23.829
that automation is something that happens to people

1000
00:45:23.829 --> 00:45:28.270
in car factories, and that it comes in.

1001
00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:30.510
You come into work one day, and there's a machine there,

1002
00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:33.759
and sorry, Rich, your job has been automated.

1003
00:45:33.759 --> 00:45:36.540
Have fun finding new work.

1004
00:45:36.540 --> 00:45:39.620
That's not really how it works anymore most of the time.

1005
00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:42.388
And that's not who it happens to most of the time.

1006
00:45:42.388 --> 00:45:45.340
Most people who are being displaced by AI and automation

1007
00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:47.807
are not manufacturing workers.

1008
00:45:47.807 --> 00:45:49.390
They're people in retail.

1009
00:45:49.390 --> 00:45:50.970
They're people doing clerical work,

1010
00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.530
and they're increasingly white collar professionals,

1011
00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:55.789
like, doing the kinds of things that you and I do.

1012
00:45:55.789 --> 00:46:00.789
People in sales, people in finance, people in medicine,

1013
00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:03.188
people in law.

1014
00:46:03.188 --> 00:46:04.810
There are now algorithms

1015
00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:07.469
that can diagnose certain types of cancers more accurately

1016
00:46:07.469 --> 00:46:10.060
than human radiologists.

1017
00:46:10.060 --> 00:46:11.710
So it's not just like truck drivers,

1018
00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:16.499
and McDonald's cashiers and factory workers.

1019
00:46:16.499 --> 00:46:19.290
And so I think that requires us to sort of

1020
00:46:19.290 --> 00:46:21.537
update our mental model of what this stuff is,

1021
00:46:21.537 --> 00:46:23.837
and how it's going to affect our lives

1022
00:46:23.837 --> 00:46:26.608
because for a long time this was sort of like

1023
00:46:26.608 --> 00:46:29.668
people like me thought this is someone else's problem.

1024
00:46:29.668 --> 00:46:33.563
Uh mm, uh mm, and with that,

1025
00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:36.630
from my perspective,

1026
00:46:36.630 --> 00:46:39.300
it's almost that nobody is immune, right?

1027
00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:44.180
Like, you talk about how one of the kind of catalysts

1028
00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:45.505
for this was understanding

1029
00:46:45.505 --> 00:46:48.860
early in your career when you were a financial reporter,

1030
00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:52.890
that a lot of those articles are now generated by AI.

1031
00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:54.220
There isn't even a journalist

1032
00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:56.547
that writes these financial report documents.

1033
00:46:56.547 --> 00:47:01.547
So if that could be the case then is anybody safe?

1034
00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:05.880
And was it last week where there was the whole

1035
00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:08.060
Tom Cruise deepfake thing on TikTok.

1036
00:47:08.060 --> 00:47:10.090
Like even Tom Cruise is not immune

1037
00:47:10.090 --> 00:47:13.828
from being automated into movies.

1038
00:47:13.828 --> 00:47:16.860
So it is frightening.

1039
00:47:16.860 --> 00:47:18.576
And it does beg the question of, like,

1040
00:47:18.576 --> 00:47:20.410
what are we gonna do about this?

1041
00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:23.453
But at the same time kind of balancing out against that,

1042
00:47:23.453 --> 00:47:27.008
you also see these AI generated screenplays

1043
00:47:27.008 --> 00:47:28.300
that are terrible,

1044
00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:32.040
or the point of sale machine at the grocery store

1045
00:47:32.040 --> 00:47:33.080
that never works right.

1046
00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:35.110
Like a lot of this stuff is super janky.

1047
00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:37.812
Like, it feels like it's super futuristic,

1048
00:47:37.812 --> 00:47:41.862
and, also, completely ineffectual at the same time.

1049
00:47:41.862 --> 00:47:44.654
Yeah, there's a great concept

1050
00:47:44.654 --> 00:47:47.743
that I got from these two economists,

1051
00:47:47.743 --> 00:47:50.323
Daron Acemoglu, and Pascual Restrepo,

1052
00:47:50.323 --> 00:47:53.930
who write about automation and its affects on labor markets.

1053
00:47:53.930 --> 00:47:56.671
And they talk about this concept of so-so automation,

1054
00:47:56.671 --> 00:47:59.980
which is sort of the janky automation you're talking about.

1055
00:47:59.980 --> 00:48:03.430
It's like the grocery store self-checkout thing,

1056
00:48:03.430 --> 00:48:05.520
or like the call center,

1057
00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:07.350
you call for customer service

1058
00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:11.613
when you need to like renew your insurance, or something.

1059
00:48:11.613 --> 00:48:14.730
And like, it's the kind of automation

1060
00:48:14.730 --> 00:48:18.150
that actually isn't much better if it's at all better

1061
00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:21.100
than the humans it replaced but it is cheaper.

1062
00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:23.821
And it allows companies to reduce their costs,

1063
00:48:23.821 --> 00:48:26.470
but it doesn't, like, make them, I mean,

1064
00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:30.200
in the past what's happened is we've gotten technology

1065
00:48:30.200 --> 00:48:31.880
that displaces some old jobs,

1066
00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:34.310
but it also creates new jobs, right?

1067
00:48:34.310 --> 00:48:39.310
The car, the automobile displaced a lot of carriage drivers,

1068
00:48:40.180 --> 00:48:41.670
and people who sold buggy whips,

1069
00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:43.260
and blacksmiths who made horseshoes,

1070
00:48:43.260 --> 00:48:45.770
but suddenly you could get a job as a chauffeur,

1071
00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:47.752
or a car dealer, or a mechanic.

1072
00:48:47.752 --> 00:48:49.440
And so that's kind of the way

1073
00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:51.840
that we've been able to kind of keep the economy running,

1074
00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:53.792
even as people get displaced,

1075
00:48:53.792 --> 00:48:56.750
but these new kinds of automation

1076
00:48:56.750 --> 00:48:58.120
that we're seeing a lot more of,

1077
00:48:58.120 --> 00:49:00.730
these kind of so-so forms of automation

1078
00:49:00.730 --> 00:49:03.770
they're basically just good enough to replace humans,

1079
00:49:03.770 --> 00:49:07.500
but not good enough to enable other kinds of work,

1080
00:49:07.500 --> 00:49:10.630
and to grow productivity, and to grow the economy.

1081
00:49:10.630 --> 00:49:14.040
And so the solution is actually not less automation,

1082
00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:15.360
it's better automation.

1083
00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:17.750
Right, you talk about the productivity paradox.

1084
00:49:17.750 --> 00:49:21.508
Like if these innovations in AI were so good

1085
00:49:21.508 --> 00:49:24.820
then how come we're not infinitely more productive

1086
00:49:24.820 --> 00:49:25.690
than we were before?

1087
00:49:25.690 --> 00:49:26.830
We're kind of the same.

1088
00:49:26.830 --> 00:49:30.800
And what's qualitatively different about this moment is that

1089
00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:34.600
the AI that we're onboarding at the moment

1090
00:49:34.600 --> 00:49:39.600
isn't having that reciprocal impact on creating new jobs.

1091
00:49:39.770 --> 00:49:41.030
It's eliminating jobs,

1092
00:49:41.030 --> 00:49:43.926
but we're not seeing the opportunities

1093
00:49:43.926 --> 00:49:47.338
that generally come with new innovations,

1094
00:49:47.338 --> 00:49:48.700
and that's kind of

1095
00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:50.860
the hearkening of something very different.

1096
00:49:50.860 --> 00:49:52.899
Totally, yeah, there's the whole industry

1097
00:49:52.899 --> 00:49:55.150
that's grown up in the last 10 years,

1098
00:49:55.150 --> 00:49:59.378
and it's called RPA, it's robotic process automation.

1099
00:49:59.378 --> 00:50:04.150
And this is basically companies that sell software

1100
00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:06.228
to replace humans in the workplace.

1101
00:50:06.228 --> 00:50:08.620
So they're companies you've never heard of,

1102
00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:12.090
like UiPath, and Automation Anywhere, and Blue Prism.

1103
00:50:12.090 --> 00:50:15.556
They're kind of these faceless like B2B software companies,

1104
00:50:15.556 --> 00:50:18.150
but they're huge, and they're making tons of money.

1105
00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:20.987
And the way that they're doing that is by selling Coca-Cola,

1106
00:50:20.987 --> 00:50:23.650
or Walgreens a software package,

1107
00:50:23.650 --> 00:50:26.700
and saying we can do with one bot,

1108
00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:30.066
what used to take 20 people in billing to do.

1109
00:50:30.066 --> 00:50:33.560
And so executives love this stuff 'cause it's cheap,

1110
00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:35.323
and it's easy, and it's efficient.

1111
00:50:35.323 --> 00:50:38.290
And even if it's not quite as good as the humans

1112
00:50:38.290 --> 00:50:39.230
who used to do that job,

1113
00:50:39.230 --> 00:50:40.530
even if it makes some mistakes,

1114
00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:42.300
and you have to kind of come in and correct it,

1115
00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:43.890
you're still lowering your costs.

1116
00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:45.970
And so that's what they're going after.

1117
00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:49.320
And that industry, I think, flies under their people's radar

1118
00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:50.440
'cause it's not flashy.

1119
00:50:50.440 --> 00:50:53.900
It's not Siri and Alexa, it's not flying cars.

1120
00:50:53.900 --> 00:50:57.050
It's not self-driving Ubers.

1121
00:50:57.050 --> 00:51:01.240
It's this very basic office automation software,

1122
00:51:01.240 --> 00:51:03.330
but it's had a huge impact on the labor market.

1123
00:51:03.330 --> 00:51:04.163
Right, and, of course,

1124
00:51:04.163 --> 00:51:07.130
the pandemic has presented the opportune moment

1125
00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:11.245
to implement a lot of these automated systems,

1126
00:51:11.245 --> 00:51:14.220
while everybody's working from home,

1127
00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:17.320
and to do it in kind of a guiltless manner.

1128
00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:19.327
So talk a little bit about how COVID

1129
00:51:19.327 --> 00:51:22.830
has accelerated this process.

1130
00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:25.410
Well, COVID has really transformed

1131
00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:28.440
the way that executives feel about automation.

1132
00:51:28.440 --> 00:51:29.620
That has been the biggest thing.

1133
00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:31.500
I mean, there are certainly companies

1134
00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:34.810
that have used automation to kind of get through COVID.

1135
00:51:34.810 --> 00:51:39.327
So meat packing plants, FedEx, shipping companies,

1136
00:51:39.327 --> 00:51:43.060
they've had to sort of bring in robotics and automation

1137
00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:45.990
just to be able to kind of keep up with demand

1138
00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:48.118
while some of their humans are out sick,

1139
00:51:48.118 --> 00:51:51.100
but there's this other piece of this which is that

1140
00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:53.460
I think for a long time executives knew

1141
00:51:53.460 --> 00:51:55.197
that they could automate people,

1142
00:51:55.197 --> 00:51:57.890
but they were hesitant to do it

1143
00:51:57.890 --> 00:52:01.054
because they didn't want to be seen as job killers.

1144
00:52:01.054 --> 00:52:05.163
They saw what happened to executives in the '90s

1145
00:52:05.163 --> 00:52:09.045
who sent jobs, who outsourced jobs to India and China,

1146
00:52:09.045 --> 00:52:10.990
they became villains,

1147
00:52:10.990 --> 00:52:15.060
and they were sort of the bad guys in global capitalism.

1148
00:52:15.060 --> 00:52:17.220
And so I think a lot of them sort of hesitated.

1149
00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:20.510
They said, well, let's try to do a little bit of automation,

1150
00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:21.700
but like maybe not the kind

1151
00:52:21.700 --> 00:52:25.390
that's gonna replace anyone, and we'll sort of play it safe.

1152
00:52:25.390 --> 00:52:27.120
And then COVID happened.

1153
00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:28.970
And it was like someone described it to me,

1154
00:52:28.970 --> 00:52:30.500
a consultant who helps companies

1155
00:52:30.500 --> 00:52:33.754
sort of do this kind of automation said, it's basically,

1156
00:52:33.754 --> 00:52:36.540
the executives are saying like we don't care anymore.

1157
00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:39.550
Like we just need to do what we need to do for our business.

1158
00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:41.190
And he said, it's made automation

1159
00:52:41.190 --> 00:52:42.840
a lot more politically acceptable

1160
00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:44.880
because tons of people are out of work.

1161
00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:46.130
Businesses are shutting down.

1162
00:52:46.130 --> 00:52:48.880
And so people, I think, are a lot more

1163
00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:50.550
sort of forgiving of companies

1164
00:52:50.550 --> 00:52:53.183
that need to make these changes right now.

1165
00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:56.160
So the flood gates have kind of been opened in a way.

1166
00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:57.110
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1167
00:52:57.110 --> 00:53:00.702
Before we get into the prescriptive aspect of all of this

1168
00:53:00.702 --> 00:53:05.250
was there anything that surprised you in all the research

1169
00:53:05.250 --> 00:53:06.190
that you've done?

1170
00:53:06.190 --> 00:53:09.080
I mean, obviously you're very tech fluent,

1171
00:53:09.080 --> 00:53:10.730
and savvy going into this,

1172
00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:12.710
but what was something

1173
00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:15.193
that you didn't expect that you learned?

1174
00:53:16.580 --> 00:53:19.940
Well, I had sort of bought this narrative

1175
00:53:19.940 --> 00:53:22.500
that I had heard from people in Silicon Valley,

1176
00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:24.960
which is that basically every time

1177
00:53:24.960 --> 00:53:28.540
there's like a big tech transformation,

1178
00:53:28.540 --> 00:53:30.440
whether it's the first Industrial Revolution,

1179
00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:32.943
or the invention of electricity,

1180
00:53:32.943 --> 00:53:37.943
or the kind of factory automation of the 1960s and '70s,

1181
00:53:37.953 --> 00:53:40.270
that this has gone pretty smoothly.

1182
00:53:40.270 --> 00:53:41.440
Like that's the sort of story

1183
00:53:41.440 --> 00:53:43.730
that people tell out here in the Bay Area is, like,

1184
00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:44.682
yeah, there were some farmers

1185
00:53:44.682 --> 00:53:47.120
who had to learn how to work in factories,

1186
00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:50.730
but like, overall, this improved everyone's lives.

1187
00:53:50.730 --> 00:53:51.700
People were happy

1188
00:53:51.700 --> 00:53:53.712
that they no longer had to do the old jobs.

1189
00:53:53.712 --> 00:53:55.760
And so I went back,

1190
00:53:55.760 --> 00:53:57.910
and I actually read a lot of the primary sources,

1191
00:53:57.910 --> 00:54:01.261
like people's journals from the Industrial Revolution,

1192
00:54:01.261 --> 00:54:02.490
things like that.

1193
00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:05.510
And, like, it sucked for a lot of people.

1194
00:54:05.510 --> 00:54:10.370
Like, this was not a happy time in a lot of worker's lives

1195
00:54:10.370 --> 00:54:11.409
because all of a sudden,

1196
00:54:11.409 --> 00:54:15.290
they had to learn new skills, go to a new place to work,

1197
00:54:15.290 --> 00:54:17.640
deal with these, like, awful working conditions.

1198
00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:19.010
There's child labor,

1199
00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:21.890
there's exploitation on a massive scale.

1200
00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:24.826
And worker's wages actually didn't reflect

1201
00:54:24.826 --> 00:54:27.305
the increased productivity.

1202
00:54:27.305 --> 00:54:29.930
Corporate owners and factory owners

1203
00:54:29.930 --> 00:54:32.735
were making a ton more money because of all the automation,

1204
00:54:32.735 --> 00:54:36.340
but workers weren't actually seeing a lot of that.

1205
00:54:36.340 --> 00:54:39.800
And this happened, again, with electricity,

1206
00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:44.420
and again with factory automation in the 1960s and '70s.

1207
00:54:44.420 --> 00:54:48.230
There's a great book by this economist named Carl Frey,

1208
00:54:48.230 --> 00:54:50.271
called "The Technology Trap"

1209
00:54:50.271 --> 00:54:55.271
and it's all about how we've kind of invented this history

1210
00:54:55.839 --> 00:54:58.650
where technology just comes in,

1211
00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:01.210
and there's a little bit of displacement.

1212
00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:02.850
It's a little rocky for a few years,

1213
00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:06.180
but then everyone moves on and we're prosperous and happy.

1214
00:55:06.180 --> 00:55:10.080
And technology is just sort of the grand march of progress.

1215
00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:11.240
And he basically says, like,

1216
00:55:11.240 --> 00:55:13.890
hmm, that's not really how it goes.

1217
00:55:13.890 --> 00:55:15.900
And his line is something like,

1218
00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:18.033
if this is just another Industrial Revolution

1219
00:55:18.033 --> 00:55:19.690
we're living through,

1220
00:55:19.690 --> 00:55:22.160
then, like, alarm bells should be ringing.

1221
00:55:22.160 --> 00:55:23.650
Like, we should be really freaked out.

1222
00:55:23.650 --> 00:55:27.253
Right, yeah, it's terrifying.

1223
00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:30.380
But there is a hopeful,

1224
00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:32.270
like, I want to make it clear that, like,

1225
00:55:32.270 --> 00:55:35.360
this is not a book that's like all doom and gloom

1226
00:55:35.360 --> 00:55:38.020
because I don't actually think we are screwed.

1227
00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:39.580
And in fact, I think that

1228
00:55:42.037 --> 00:55:43.560
we know from history,

1229
00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:45.690
and from just looking at our environment,

1230
00:55:45.690 --> 00:55:47.640
like what we need to do to survive,

1231
00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:49.493
and to be happy and successful.

1232
00:55:51.400 --> 00:55:53.160
This is not destiny.

1233
00:55:53.160 --> 00:55:55.720
We don't have to end up suffering in the same way

1234
00:55:55.720 --> 00:55:57.680
that people have when they've been displaced

1235
00:55:57.680 --> 00:55:58.513
throughout history,

1236
00:55:58.513 --> 00:56:00.610
because we actually know what to do to prevent that.

1237
00:56:00.610 --> 00:56:02.730
Right, and the solution,

1238
00:56:02.730 --> 00:56:05.570
the thesis that you lay out in the book is essentially

1239
00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:10.370
to make yourself, I don't know, bulletproof,

1240
00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:14.900
but to protect yourself against this rising tide.

1241
00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:18.130
That solution resides in being the most human

1242
00:56:18.130 --> 00:56:19.450
that we can possibly be.

1243
00:56:19.450 --> 00:56:23.600
It's about cultivating our inner humanity, which is

1244
00:56:25.942 --> 00:56:29.570
a thesis in contradiction to a more conventional narrative,

1245
00:56:29.570 --> 00:56:31.560
which is if you want to be competitive in this world

1246
00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:32.640
it's all about STEM.

1247
00:56:32.640 --> 00:56:33.891
You got to learn how to code.

1248
00:56:33.891 --> 00:56:36.264
You have to be computer proficient,

1249
00:56:36.264 --> 00:56:38.530
but those, as you point out in the book,

1250
00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:42.597
like all of those pursuits are the first to be automated,

1251
00:56:42.597 --> 00:56:46.740
and a terrain in which a human is very ill-equipped

1252
00:56:46.740 --> 00:56:49.900
to compete with a supercomputer.

1253
00:56:49.900 --> 00:56:52.560
Yeah, that's a really perceptive reading.

1254
00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:54.253
And I think that's right.

1255
00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:57.250
For a long time we've been training people

1256
00:56:57.250 --> 00:56:58.730
to effectively be machines.

1257
00:56:58.730 --> 00:57:01.360
We've been telling them major in engineering,

1258
00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:03.770
learn to code, optimize your life,

1259
00:57:03.770 --> 00:57:05.760
like if there's anything that's inefficient,

1260
00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:08.254
or suboptimal about your life,

1261
00:57:08.254 --> 00:57:11.825
take that out and get as much done as you can.

1262
00:57:11.825 --> 00:57:14.320
All this hustle culture we see

1263
00:57:14.320 --> 00:57:17.750
is like very much a product of that sort of attitude about

1264
00:57:17.750 --> 00:57:19.300
what's valuable in the economy.

1265
00:57:19.300 --> 00:57:21.010
And that was true for a long time.

1266
00:57:21.010 --> 00:57:24.540
Like you could differentiate yourself through hard work,

1267
00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:27.043
and hustle and just pure effort,

1268
00:57:27.043 --> 00:57:30.175
but that's like not what's gonna happen.

1269
00:57:30.175 --> 00:57:31.850
That's not what's happening right now.

1270
00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:35.060
And we're starting to see that the skills

1271
00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:37.613
that are actually in demand right now are these kind of

1272
00:57:37.613 --> 00:57:40.600
what we would pejoratively call like soft skills.

1273
00:57:40.600 --> 00:57:42.900
It's things like communication and empathy,

1274
00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:45.056
and leadership and courage,

1275
00:57:45.056 --> 00:57:47.624
the things that machines can't do.

1276
00:57:47.624 --> 00:57:51.450
You're never gonna be able to out hustle an algorithm.

1277
00:57:51.450 --> 00:57:54.050
Like it's just not possible.

1278
00:57:54.050 --> 00:57:55.810
They can work 24 hours a day,

1279
00:57:55.810 --> 00:57:57.990
and do millions of calculations a second,

1280
00:57:57.990 --> 00:57:59.610
and you're just never gonna keep up.

1281
00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:02.900
And so the value is going to shift

1282
00:58:02.900 --> 00:58:05.810
from the sort of effort-based things

1283
00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:08.610
to the kind of emotional and social things.

1284
00:58:08.610 --> 00:58:09.443
And in the book,

1285
00:58:09.443 --> 00:58:11.970
I talk about three sort of categories of work

1286
00:58:11.970 --> 00:58:14.520
that I think are gonna be really resistant to automation.

1287
00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:16.930
And I think that's sort of where people should go.

1288
00:58:16.930 --> 00:58:19.680
That being surprising, social, and scarce,

1289
00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:21.690
which is, basically, your first tenet

1290
00:58:21.690 --> 00:58:24.600
in this list of nine things.

1291
00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:25.440
Yeah, you got it.

1292
00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:27.110
So those are the three types of work

1293
00:58:27.110 --> 00:58:28.910
that I think is gonna be hard to automate.

1294
00:58:28.910 --> 00:58:31.521
And this was drawn from interviews

1295
00:58:31.521 --> 00:58:34.020
with tons of AI executives.

1296
00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:36.280
This was not just me sort of sitting in my office

1297
00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:38.390
like dreaming up a strategy.

1298
00:58:38.390 --> 00:58:41.237
And this is based on a ton of reporting.

1299
00:58:41.237 --> 00:58:44.750
And the things that I asked AI experts were basically

1300
00:58:44.750 --> 00:58:48.091
what are humans good at that machines right now are bad at?

1301
00:58:48.091 --> 00:58:49.920
And they told me that that basically

1302
00:58:49.920 --> 00:58:51.480
falls into these three categories,

1303
00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:53.391
surprising, social, and scarce.

1304
00:58:53.391 --> 00:58:56.020
So let's just take them one at a time.

1305
00:58:56.020 --> 00:58:58.930
So surprising is sort of like work that involves kind of

1306
00:58:58.930 --> 00:59:02.938
irregular environments, changing rules, lots of variables,

1307
00:59:02.938 --> 00:59:05.410
things that aren't predictable or regular.

1308
00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:07.430
Look, AI really likes rules,

1309
00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:10.150
and bounded environments, and structure.

1310
00:59:10.150 --> 00:59:12.112
And so that's why it's really good at playing chess,

1311
00:59:12.112 --> 00:59:13.260
for example,

1312
00:59:13.260 --> 00:59:16.640
because every game of chess has exactly the same rules,

1313
00:59:16.640 --> 00:59:21.108
but if you train an AI to be a kindergarten teacher,

1314
00:59:21.108 --> 00:59:24.060
like, it's not gonna do so well, right?

1315
00:59:24.060 --> 00:59:26.228
Like, it's gonna be pretty bad at that.

1316
00:59:26.228 --> 00:59:29.488
And that's because there's so many environments,

1317
00:59:29.488 --> 00:59:31.160
there's so many variables,

1318
00:59:31.160 --> 00:59:34.720
there's so much going wrong at any given time.

1319
00:59:34.720 --> 00:59:37.880
You have to be really adaptable and flexible to do that job.

1320
00:59:37.880 --> 00:59:40.523
And so that's for now that's human work.

1321
00:59:41.540 --> 00:59:46.324
Social jobs are jobs that involve making people feel things

1322
00:59:46.324 --> 00:59:48.670
rather than making things.

1323
00:59:48.670 --> 00:59:53.040
So this is all the sort of compassionate jobs we think of,

1324
00:59:53.040 --> 00:59:58.040
social workers, home care workers, ministers, therapists,

1325
00:59:58.150 --> 00:59:59.230
people like that,

1326
00:59:59.230 --> 01:00:01.280
but it also includes things that you wouldn't think of

1327
01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:05.713
as being social jobs, like baristas, for example.

1328
01:00:06.900 --> 01:00:08.700
On one level that's a job

1329
01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:10.360
that got automated a long time ago.

1330
01:00:10.360 --> 01:00:12.320
We have machines in our houses

1331
01:00:12.320 --> 01:00:14.690
that make perfectly good coffee,

1332
01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:17.080
but we still like to go to, I mean, pre-COVID,

1333
01:00:17.080 --> 01:00:18.811
we still like to go to coffee shops

1334
01:00:18.811 --> 01:00:21.550
because there's a human element there.

1335
01:00:21.550 --> 01:00:23.400
We're getting something out of that transaction

1336
01:00:23.400 --> 01:00:25.900
that's more than just a cup of coffee.

1337
01:00:25.900 --> 01:00:28.940
So those kinds of jobs are gonna be hard to automate

1338
01:00:28.940 --> 01:00:32.317
because machines aren't that good at making us feel things,

1339
01:00:32.317 --> 01:00:33.971
and people are much better at that.

1340
01:00:33.971 --> 01:00:37.580
And then the final category is scarce jobs,

1341
01:00:37.580 --> 01:00:39.941
which is sort of people who have rare skills

1342
01:00:39.941 --> 01:00:43.140
are excellent in their fields.

1343
01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:44.800
People who are sort of working

1344
01:00:44.800 --> 01:00:47.500
in like low fault tolerant environments.

1345
01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:50.220
So people like EMTs,

1346
01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:53.350
and people who answer the phone when you call 911.

1347
01:00:53.350 --> 01:00:55.020
Those jobs are gonna be very hard to automate.

1348
01:00:55.020 --> 01:00:56.860
Right, when I think about

1349
01:00:58.930 --> 01:01:01.930
you and your career, I see somebody who

1350
01:01:04.020 --> 01:01:08.260
created a situation in which you differentiated yourself

1351
01:01:08.260 --> 01:01:09.093
from your peers.

1352
01:01:09.093 --> 01:01:12.870
Like you, on some level you've created a personal brand

1353
01:01:12.870 --> 01:01:14.677
around the kind of beat that you're on,

1354
01:01:14.677 --> 01:01:16.865
and the stories that you tell.

1355
01:01:16.865 --> 01:01:20.840
And I just know as somebody who follows you,

1356
01:01:20.840 --> 01:01:23.950
yes, I have reverence for "The New York Times,"

1357
01:01:23.950 --> 01:01:26.560
and that means something,

1358
01:01:26.560 --> 01:01:29.500
but I'm not opening up "The New York Times" app

1359
01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:32.860
to see what "The New York Times" has to say about subject X.

1360
01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:35.640
It's like I want to know what Kevin thinks about this,

1361
01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:38.180
or I want to know what Taylor Lorenz thinks about

1362
01:01:38.180 --> 01:01:40.030
this other internet thing.

1363
01:01:40.030 --> 01:01:43.250
Whether it's Nicholas Kristof, or David Brooks,

1364
01:01:43.250 --> 01:01:44.860
or whoever it is,

1365
01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:49.860
this idea that you inject your work with your personality.

1366
01:01:50.310 --> 01:01:54.060
And that becomes so embedded in what you do

1367
01:01:54.060 --> 01:01:57.848
that some AI could cover a subject matter,

1368
01:01:57.848 --> 01:02:00.970
but it wouldn't have the imprimatur of what you do.

1369
01:02:00.970 --> 01:02:04.880
And so you create career safety for yourself

1370
01:02:04.880 --> 01:02:06.233
by carving that out.

1371
01:02:07.650 --> 01:02:08.680
Yeah, and I want to be clear,

1372
01:02:08.680 --> 01:02:11.590
like, this was not an intentional strategy on my part.

1373
01:02:11.590 --> 01:02:14.560
Like, I did not think, like, I am gonna run from the robots

1374
01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:19.560
by starting podcasts, and writing newsletters,

1375
01:02:20.120 --> 01:02:22.120
and being on Twitter all the time, like,

1376
01:02:22.120 --> 01:02:24.650
but I think a lot of us in industries

1377
01:02:24.650 --> 01:02:27.263
that have been challenged by automation like journalism

1378
01:02:27.263 --> 01:02:31.250
have sort of subconsciously adapted in these ways

1379
01:02:31.250 --> 01:02:33.380
that make sense when we look back at them.

1380
01:02:33.380 --> 01:02:36.685
So, yeah, I did used to write formulaic stories.

1381
01:02:36.685 --> 01:02:41.120
I wrote the most boring corporate earnings reports

1382
01:02:41.120 --> 01:02:43.480
you could ever imagine, like,

1383
01:02:43.480 --> 01:02:47.920
Alcoa made $7 million in its smelting division last quarter.

1384
01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:48.910
Like, that's the kind of stuff

1385
01:02:48.910 --> 01:02:50.670
I was doing early in my career.

1386
01:02:50.670 --> 01:02:52.890
And then I sort of started realizing, like,

1387
01:02:52.890 --> 01:02:53.810
I need to kind of like

1388
01:02:53.810 --> 01:02:56.280
put a little bit more of myself into my work

1389
01:02:56.280 --> 01:02:58.250
because otherwise I'm replaceable,

1390
01:02:58.250 --> 01:03:02.380
not necessarily by an AI, but just by another person.

1391
01:03:02.380 --> 01:03:06.180
And so I started infusing more of my personality into stuff,

1392
01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:08.970
and I think that's what's happening in the media

1393
01:03:08.970 --> 01:03:12.390
writ large right now is you're starting to see the kind of

1394
01:03:12.390 --> 01:03:15.540
it's almost like moving from like an industrial economy

1395
01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:17.440
to an artisanal economy.

1396
01:03:17.440 --> 01:03:19.234
It's sort of like we used to be these factories,

1397
01:03:19.234 --> 01:03:22.850
newspapers used to be these factories that churned out,

1398
01:03:22.850 --> 01:03:25.020
like, pink slime, essentially.

1399
01:03:25.020 --> 01:03:27.870
And like all content was sort of the institutional,

1400
01:03:27.870 --> 01:03:29.487
like, voice from God.

1401
01:03:29.487 --> 01:03:30.480
And it was like,

1402
01:03:30.480 --> 01:03:32.870
it didn't matter really who the by-lines were

1403
01:03:32.870 --> 01:03:35.096
'cause that wasn't really what people opened it for.

1404
01:03:35.096 --> 01:03:37.220
And now you're starting to see kind of like,

1405
01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:39.780
what I call, like, artisanal content making,

1406
01:03:39.780 --> 01:03:43.708
which is like I am one person, you are following me.

1407
01:03:43.708 --> 01:03:46.487
This is my opinion, this is my reporting.

1408
01:03:46.487 --> 01:03:47.900
I'm not gonna hide the fact

1409
01:03:47.900 --> 01:03:50.351
that I am a person who made this.

1410
01:03:50.351 --> 01:03:52.740
And that's, I think where we're headed,

1411
01:03:52.740 --> 01:03:54.660
not just in journalism, but in a lot of industries.

1412
01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:57.950
Right, and it plays out not just in individuals,

1413
01:03:57.950 --> 01:04:00.780
but in corporations as well.

1414
01:04:00.780 --> 01:04:03.553
Like you want to find that cool shop

1415
01:04:03.553 --> 01:04:07.134
that's on the main drag of the groovy town

1416
01:04:07.134 --> 01:04:10.030
that is not part of

1417
01:04:10.030 --> 01:04:15.030
the Pottery Barn, Gap industrial complex, like,

1418
01:04:15.150 --> 01:04:17.470
and that's becoming harder and harder to do,

1419
01:04:17.470 --> 01:04:21.510
so then we then develop a greater sense of value

1420
01:04:21.510 --> 01:04:23.290
around those things.

1421
01:04:23.290 --> 01:04:25.144
Yeah, there's a whole chapter about this in the book.

1422
01:04:25.144 --> 01:04:29.040
I did a lot of reading around the psychology of value,

1423
01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:32.590
and why we value certain things more highly than others.

1424
01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:35.130
And there's a concept in social science

1425
01:04:35.130 --> 01:04:38.430
called the effort heuristic, and, basically, what it says

1426
01:04:38.430 --> 01:04:40.270
is that we attach value to things

1427
01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:42.443
that we think people worked really hard on.

1428
01:04:42.443 --> 01:04:44.150
So they've run experiments.

1429
01:04:44.150 --> 01:04:46.925
There's a professor at UNC who's run all these experiments

1430
01:04:46.925 --> 01:04:51.636
where he gives people a bag of candy, and he says,

1431
01:04:51.636 --> 01:04:56.510
this candy was randomly selected for you and people eat it.

1432
01:04:56.510 --> 01:04:58.080
And they give another group of people

1433
01:04:58.080 --> 01:04:59.300
the exact same bag of candy,

1434
01:04:59.300 --> 01:05:01.330
but instead of saying this was randomly selected,

1435
01:05:01.330 --> 01:05:04.116
they say a human picked this out for you,

1436
01:05:04.116 --> 01:05:08.350
specially based on your tastes, and they love it.

1437
01:05:08.350 --> 01:05:10.080
Like, it makes a big difference

1438
01:05:10.080 --> 01:05:12.550
in their appreciation of the thing

1439
01:05:12.550 --> 01:05:15.130
when they think that someone else worked hard on it.

1440
01:05:15.130 --> 01:05:17.730
So I think this is a really good roadmap

1441
01:05:17.730 --> 01:05:21.320
for like where the economy, and where our jobs are headed

1442
01:05:21.320 --> 01:05:24.695
is I think that we have this tendency to want to say, like,

1443
01:05:24.695 --> 01:05:29.290
we want to sort of remove ourselves from our work,

1444
01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:32.300
and sort of the biggest compliment you could pay someone

1445
01:05:32.300 --> 01:05:34.538
for awhile was like, oh, he makes that look so easy,

1446
01:05:34.538 --> 01:05:36.140
but I think that's actually not.

1447
01:05:36.140 --> 01:05:38.500
Like I think what we're moving to is an economy

1448
01:05:38.500 --> 01:05:42.600
in which people who say, oh, he worked so hard on that.

1449
01:05:42.600 --> 01:05:45.497
Like that becomes the thing that is valuable.

1450
01:05:45.497 --> 01:05:48.869
Everything that is done by machines will become cheap,

1451
01:05:48.869 --> 01:05:51.370
and inexpensive and easy,

1452
01:05:51.370 --> 01:05:53.750
and everything that's done by humans will be valuable,

1453
01:05:53.750 --> 01:05:56.020
and hard and will be rewarded for that hardness.

1454
01:05:56.020 --> 01:05:59.150
Right, I don't necessarily want the coffee mug

1455
01:05:59.150 --> 01:06:00.380
that I can buy at Target,

1456
01:06:00.380 --> 01:06:04.300
but I really want the one that Seth Rogen made in his house

1457
01:06:04.300 --> 01:06:05.710
on his pottery wheel.

1458
01:06:05.710 --> 01:06:10.240
I love Seth Rogen's pottery, I'm sort of obsessed, yeah.

1459
01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:11.799
No, and I think that's like,

1460
01:06:11.799 --> 01:06:13.451
so one of the rules in the book,

1461
01:06:13.451 --> 01:06:16.500
in the second half of the book is leave handprints

1462
01:06:16.500 --> 01:06:19.710
because I think we've gotten accustomed as sort of workers

1463
01:06:19.710 --> 01:06:24.680
to kind of trying to, like, make everything we do perfect,

1464
01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:26.070
and look effortless,

1465
01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:28.270
and, like, take away all traces of the fact

1466
01:06:28.270 --> 01:06:29.703
that we struggled over something,

1467
01:06:29.703 --> 01:06:33.900
but I think what we should be doing is really emphasizing

1468
01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:35.514
the humanity that goes into something,

1469
01:06:35.514 --> 01:06:38.900
emphasizing that we are people who are making things,

1470
01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:40.490
who are doing labor in the world,

1471
01:06:40.490 --> 01:06:42.660
and that that's what makes it valuable,

1472
01:06:42.660 --> 01:06:44.233
that we're not machines.

1473
01:06:44.233 --> 01:06:48.150
And there's a great talk by this guy, Yann LeCun,

1474
01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:50.883
who's the head of AI research at Facebook,

1475
01:06:50.883 --> 01:06:54.870
and he uses the example of a flat screen TV,

1476
01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:56.210
and a ceramic bowl.

1477
01:06:56.210 --> 01:06:59.230
So kind of like your Seth Rogen mug.

1478
01:06:59.230 --> 01:07:00.810
And he says flat screen TV

1479
01:07:00.810 --> 01:07:02.600
is like an amazing piece of technology.

1480
01:07:02.600 --> 01:07:04.600
It's got hundreds of parts,

1481
01:07:04.600 --> 01:07:07.129
it's got rare-earth metals, it's got lasers,

1482
01:07:07.129 --> 01:07:10.820
and it's entirely made by robots start to finish.

1483
01:07:10.820 --> 01:07:13.350
And as a result, like, you can get a pretty good TV

1484
01:07:13.350 --> 01:07:14.606
for a couple hundred bucks,

1485
01:07:14.606 --> 01:07:19.326
but a ceramic bowl that's made by, like, a talented artisan,

1486
01:07:19.326 --> 01:07:22.380
technology that's been around for thousands of years

1487
01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:25.746
that's gonna cost you probably more than the flat screen TV.

1488
01:07:25.746 --> 01:07:27.670
And the reason for that is not 'cause

1489
01:07:27.670 --> 01:07:30.160
it involves more labor, or more technology,

1490
01:07:30.160 --> 01:07:32.535
it's 'cause it's done by a human rather than a machine.

1491
01:07:32.535 --> 01:07:35.190
And he thinks that's gonna be the wave of the future.

1492
01:07:35.190 --> 01:07:36.390
And I sort of agree.

1493
01:07:36.390 --> 01:07:38.290
What's really interesting about that

1494
01:07:40.215 --> 01:07:43.240
is that we're seeing that sensibility

1495
01:07:44.705 --> 01:07:49.140
getting played out in the digital space now with NFTs.

1496
01:07:49.140 --> 01:07:51.840
Like isn't an NFT,

1497
01:07:51.840 --> 01:07:56.520
a digital version of the one-off piece of pottery?

1498
01:07:56.520 --> 01:08:01.020
Totally, I have some questions about NFTs.

1499
01:08:01.020 --> 01:08:02.260
I'm actually writing about them right now.

1500
01:08:02.260 --> 01:08:03.093
I'd love to know what you think about them.

1501
01:08:03.093 --> 01:08:06.431
I mean, I still, I barely understand it at all, so.

1502
01:08:06.431 --> 01:08:11.431
You haven't paid 200 grand for a GIF of Michael Jordan?

1503
01:08:13.080 --> 01:08:16.000
Yeah, I mean, I think there's something to this idea

1504
01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:18.790
of creating scarcity on the internet,

1505
01:08:18.790 --> 01:08:22.310
because what enables the artisan

1506
01:08:22.310 --> 01:08:25.540
to charge hundreds of dollars for that ceramic bowl

1507
01:08:25.540 --> 01:08:27.199
is that there's only one of them, right?

1508
01:08:27.199 --> 01:08:31.392
And you can't duplicate it a million times for free.

1509
01:08:31.392 --> 01:08:32.433
So I think that's.

1510
01:08:32.433 --> 01:08:36.322
You could, sorry to interject, but you could 3D print it,

1511
01:08:36.322 --> 01:08:40.060
and create an exact replica of that

1512
01:08:40.060 --> 01:08:43.758
in the way that you could copy a JPEG, or a GIF,

1513
01:08:43.758 --> 01:08:45.700
but there's still the understanding

1514
01:08:45.700 --> 01:08:47.423
that it's not the original.

1515
01:08:48.290 --> 01:08:50.090
Right, and there's some friction involved in that.

1516
01:08:50.090 --> 01:08:54.230
It's not a simple process, or it's not free and infinite,

1517
01:08:54.230 --> 01:08:56.986
the way that copying a file is.

1518
01:08:56.986 --> 01:08:59.108
So I think there is value in creating

1519
01:08:59.108 --> 01:09:01.580
a model for scarcity on the internet.

1520
01:09:01.580 --> 01:09:03.297
I don't know whether NFTs are it.

1521
01:09:03.297 --> 01:09:06.120
There's been some reporting on sort of

1522
01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:08.640
the environmental questions around NFTs,

1523
01:09:08.640 --> 01:09:10.887
and whether we should be burning all this carbon

1524
01:09:10.887 --> 01:09:13.310
to like produce these.

1525
01:09:13.310 --> 01:09:16.460
I love there was a tweet the other day, it said, like, yes,

1526
01:09:16.460 --> 01:09:21.460
I left 11 Ford F-150s idling for 15 hours,

1527
01:09:21.780 --> 01:09:25.440
but now the computer says I own a drawing of Spider-Man.

1528
01:09:25.440 --> 01:09:27.230
Right.
Which I loved.

1529
01:09:27.230 --> 01:09:28.950
I don't understand why

1530
01:09:28.950 --> 01:09:30.920
so much carbon has to be burned for these,

1531
01:09:30.920 --> 01:09:32.683
but, obviously, there's a reason

1532
01:09:32.683 --> 01:09:34.560
that has to do with blockchain,

1533
01:09:34.560 --> 01:09:36.860
and stuff that I don't understand.

1534
01:09:36.860 --> 01:09:38.880
Right, so, yeah, I'll figure that out.

1535
01:09:38.880 --> 01:09:41.330
I'll come back and tell you all about it, but I'm actually,

1536
01:09:41.330 --> 01:09:44.740
like, I'm glad that people are working on this

1537
01:09:44.740 --> 01:09:47.442
because I think that one aspect,

1538
01:09:47.442 --> 01:09:50.870
one precondition for this kind of change in the economy

1539
01:09:50.870 --> 01:09:53.520
where we're moving away from mass production,

1540
01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:55.401
and toward sort of artisan production,

1541
01:09:55.401 --> 01:09:58.630
is that you need to be able to protect scarcity,

1542
01:09:58.630 --> 01:10:01.414
and protect artists and get them paid

1543
01:10:01.414 --> 01:10:06.414
for doing these kind of specialized works.

1544
01:10:06.935 --> 01:10:09.610
And so right now that doesn't really exist on the internet.

1545
01:10:09.610 --> 01:10:11.146
So I'm hopeful about NFTs.

1546
01:10:11.146 --> 01:10:12.880
Maybe I'm a sucker,

1547
01:10:12.880 --> 01:10:16.490
but I feel like it could be good for people, people like us.

1548
01:10:16.490 --> 01:10:20.330
Well, I think we're just at the starting gate of something

1549
01:10:20.330 --> 01:10:22.957
that I think could fundamentally change how we think about

1550
01:10:22.957 --> 01:10:25.610
ownership and creation.

1551
01:10:25.610 --> 01:10:28.880
We're seeing it now with JPEGs and pieces of art,

1552
01:10:28.880 --> 01:10:33.070
but it doesn't take a huge mental leap to see

1553
01:10:33.070 --> 01:10:36.930
how that impacts essentially everything that gets created.

1554
01:10:36.930 --> 01:10:40.170
I mean, Kevin, you could write a short story, or a book,

1555
01:10:40.170 --> 01:10:42.900
make that an NFT, and do a limited run.

1556
01:10:42.900 --> 01:10:47.730
Like, anything that you produce that is of you

1557
01:10:47.730 --> 01:10:51.000
can be valued in a way that's very different

1558
01:10:51.000 --> 01:10:54.290
than how we think about ownership, and

1559
01:10:56.800 --> 01:10:59.260
demand currently.

1560
01:10:59.260 --> 01:11:00.340
Yeah, I'm thinking about it.

1561
01:11:00.340 --> 01:11:02.404
Should I turn this book into an NFT?

1562
01:11:02.404 --> 01:11:04.360
I think you should. I think that would be, well, I mean,

1563
01:11:04.360 --> 01:11:06.460
you're the perfect person to do it, right?

1564
01:11:07.530 --> 01:11:11.890
It could be like this meta Charlie Kaufmanesque

1565
01:11:11.890 --> 01:11:13.900
sort of adaptation experience

1566
01:11:13.900 --> 01:11:17.660
of, like, exploring the world of NFTs through

1567
01:11:19.263 --> 01:11:24.130
a work, a written work that is itself an NFT.

1568
01:11:25.120 --> 01:11:26.680
Yeah, I'll have to talk to Random House.

1569
01:11:26.680 --> 01:11:29.320
I'm not sure how copyrights work with NFTs.

1570
01:11:29.320 --> 01:11:30.575
Well, I seriously doubt.

1571
01:11:30.575 --> 01:11:31.423
I might get a call from the lawyers.

1572
01:11:31.423 --> 01:11:34.583
That publishing houses have any concept of this right now.

1573
01:11:36.070 --> 01:11:38.440
You don't think they're talking to Malcolm Gladwell about

1574
01:11:38.440 --> 01:11:40.510
his next book-length NFT.

1575
01:11:40.510 --> 01:11:42.920
I don't know, they should be.

1576
01:11:42.920 --> 01:11:44.693
Whether they are or not, who knows?

1577
01:11:44.693 --> 01:11:46.652
I mean, I've thought about it.

1578
01:11:46.652 --> 01:11:49.350
I'm really intrigued by this.

1579
01:11:49.350 --> 01:11:52.360
And I think it could be the answer to something

1580
01:11:52.360 --> 01:11:54.680
that artists and creators have been looking for

1581
01:11:54.680 --> 01:11:57.180
for a long time because right now the way that artists,

1582
01:11:57.180 --> 01:12:00.240
and creators get paid, most of them, on the internet,

1583
01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:02.770
is that they put their content into these giant machines

1584
01:12:02.770 --> 01:12:05.510
run by algorithms that we call YouTube and Instagram,

1585
01:12:05.510 --> 01:12:07.003
and Facebook and Twitter.

1586
01:12:08.690 --> 01:12:10.470
The platform sell ads against them.

1587
01:12:10.470 --> 01:12:12.050
And then they kick back, sometimes,

1588
01:12:12.050 --> 01:12:16.010
some small percentage of what they're making to the creators

1589
01:12:16.010 --> 01:12:18.234
who have no control over what that percentage is,

1590
01:12:18.234 --> 01:12:20.920
and no control over the algorithm.

1591
01:12:20.920 --> 01:12:24.080
And they are basically like serfs,

1592
01:12:24.080 --> 01:12:27.002
in the, like, feudal empires

1593
01:12:27.002 --> 01:12:30.000
that these platforms have built.

1594
01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:32.550
And so I think any model that sort of allows people

1595
01:12:32.550 --> 01:12:36.340
to kind of cut out those middlemen is a good one.

1596
01:12:36.340 --> 01:12:37.933
And I probably shouldn't say that 'cause I am,

1597
01:12:37.933 --> 01:12:39.440
I work for a middleman.

1598
01:12:39.440 --> 01:12:42.760
Like, I'm part of the industrial content model here

1599
01:12:42.760 --> 01:12:43.660
at "The New York Times."

1600
01:12:43.660 --> 01:12:46.570
And I think there's a role for institutions,

1601
01:12:46.570 --> 01:12:48.613
and I'm not a burn it all down,

1602
01:12:49.750 --> 01:12:52.930
mass media is over, like, I don't think that's true,

1603
01:12:52.930 --> 01:12:55.150
but I do think it's good for creators

1604
01:12:55.150 --> 01:12:56.780
to have different models of getting paid.

1605
01:12:56.780 --> 01:13:01.197
Yeah, 100%, but you do have one thing in the book,

1606
01:13:02.318 --> 01:13:03.790
which is arm the rebels.

1607
01:13:03.790 --> 01:13:08.270
So there's a little bit of a punk rock aesthetic in here,

1608
01:13:08.270 --> 01:13:10.910
rise up against the machines.

1609
01:13:10.910 --> 01:13:14.020
Yeah, I mean, it's hard to talk about this stuff

1610
01:13:14.020 --> 01:13:17.218
without sounding like I'm saying technology's bad,

1611
01:13:17.218 --> 01:13:20.560
and automation's bad, and all this stuff is bad,

1612
01:13:20.560 --> 01:13:22.090
and things were better before.

1613
01:13:22.090 --> 01:13:24.540
Like, I'm not like nostalgic like that.

1614
01:13:24.540 --> 01:13:27.130
Like, I grew up on the internet, I love the internet,

1615
01:13:27.130 --> 01:13:28.939
and I want it to be the best version of itself.

1616
01:13:28.939 --> 01:13:33.130
And I also think that, like, the way that our internet

1617
01:13:33.130 --> 01:13:36.179
is arranged right now is, like, we're not doing so good.

1618
01:13:36.179 --> 01:13:41.179
And so I have this in the book I talk about these two people

1619
01:13:41.647 --> 01:13:44.738
in the 19th century.

1620
01:13:44.738 --> 01:13:48.350
One is Henry David Thoreau who wrote "Walden."

1621
01:13:48.350 --> 01:13:52.780
And he was sort of the grouchy like tech-phobe of his day.

1622
01:13:52.780 --> 01:13:54.947
He was like, this stuff all sucks.

1623
01:13:54.947 --> 01:13:59.210
I'm moving to the woods, and I'm gonna write about

1624
01:13:59.210 --> 01:14:02.470
my transcendental life in the woods.

1625
01:14:02.470 --> 01:14:05.516
And there was also this other woman very close to him

1626
01:14:05.516 --> 01:14:09.779
who is not as well-known, but her name was Sarah Bagley.

1627
01:14:09.779 --> 01:14:11.620
And she was a labor organizer.

1628
01:14:11.620 --> 01:14:12.980
She actually was one of the people

1629
01:14:12.980 --> 01:14:15.290
who worked in the factories in the Industrial Revolution,

1630
01:14:15.290 --> 01:14:17.070
and became a labor leader.

1631
01:14:17.070 --> 01:14:21.520
And she advocated for, not for she wasn't a Luddite,

1632
01:14:21.520 --> 01:14:23.530
she wasn't breaking machines,

1633
01:14:23.530 --> 01:14:25.700
but she wanted workers to be treated better.

1634
01:14:25.700 --> 01:14:27.510
She wanted the machines to produce

1635
01:14:27.510 --> 01:14:30.850
more prosperity for the people who are operating them,

1636
01:14:30.850 --> 01:14:32.650
and not just for the owners.

1637
01:14:32.650 --> 01:14:34.810
And, ultimately, she became America's

1638
01:14:34.810 --> 01:14:38.210
first female telegraph operator, which I love.

1639
01:14:38.210 --> 01:14:40.730
Like, she was sort of this groundbreaking person

1640
01:14:40.730 --> 01:14:43.760
who was part of this technological revolution.

1641
01:14:43.760 --> 01:14:46.800
She took the opposite approach.

1642
01:14:46.800 --> 01:14:48.990
She didn't run away from the technology.

1643
01:14:48.990 --> 01:14:51.460
She leaned into it and she tried to make it fairer,

1644
01:14:51.460 --> 01:14:54.160
and better, and more equitably distributed.

1645
01:14:54.160 --> 01:14:58.330
And like, I think that's the model I'm getting behind here.

1646
01:14:58.330 --> 01:14:59.970
It's not this stuff sucks,

1647
01:14:59.970 --> 01:15:01.980
and we should all throw our phones

1648
01:15:01.980 --> 01:15:04.500
in the nearest body of water and go live in the woods.

1649
01:15:04.500 --> 01:15:06.450
Like, I think we have to engage with this,

1650
01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:10.440
or else it shapes us, and we don't get to shape it.

1651
01:15:10.440 --> 01:15:12.790
Right, if you don't engage with it

1652
01:15:12.790 --> 01:15:16.340
then you truly do become a throw-esque character.

1653
01:15:16.340 --> 01:15:20.270
And it's just not an option for navigating the world.

1654
01:15:20.270 --> 01:15:21.640
Especially if you're a young person,

1655
01:15:21.640 --> 01:15:24.230
you have to be literate in these tools,

1656
01:15:24.230 --> 01:15:27.180
but it becomes incumbent upon you to shoulder

1657
01:15:27.180 --> 01:15:31.707
the responsibility of managing your life in such a way

1658
01:15:31.707 --> 01:15:34.560
that they're not commandeering your decision-making,

1659
01:15:34.560 --> 01:15:38.840
and driving you towards outcomes that you don't want.

1660
01:15:38.840 --> 01:15:40.550
So a big part of the book,

1661
01:15:40.550 --> 01:15:43.706
in addition to canvasing what the workplace

1662
01:15:43.706 --> 01:15:47.798
currently looks like, and quite possibly will look like,

1663
01:15:47.798 --> 01:15:51.880
it's about tools for how to manage

1664
01:15:51.880 --> 01:15:54.250
your own interior life, right?

1665
01:15:54.250 --> 01:15:57.190
You talk about doing these digital detoxes,

1666
01:15:57.190 --> 01:16:00.720
and learning how to sort of be in charge of your phone

1667
01:16:00.720 --> 01:16:03.220
rather than letting your phone be in charge of you.

1668
01:16:03.220 --> 01:16:05.560
So talk a little bit about what that was like

1669
01:16:05.560 --> 01:16:08.060
'cause you kind of did that experientially.

1670
01:16:08.060 --> 01:16:11.120
Yeah, so I think one hard thing.

1671
01:16:11.120 --> 01:16:14.167
So the message of the book is pretty simple it's that

1672
01:16:14.167 --> 01:16:16.820
in order to survive this wave of AI and automation,

1673
01:16:16.820 --> 01:16:19.180
and keep our jobs, and be happy, and healthy,

1674
01:16:19.180 --> 01:16:22.100
and prosperous, and good members of communities,

1675
01:16:22.100 --> 01:16:25.220
we need to become more human, pretty simple message.

1676
01:16:25.220 --> 01:16:27.620
The hard part about that is there are forces

1677
01:16:27.620 --> 01:16:29.850
that are conspiring to make us less human,

1678
01:16:29.850 --> 01:16:33.150
and some of those are right on our phone.

1679
01:16:33.150 --> 01:16:35.220
And so we interact with them everyday.

1680
01:16:35.220 --> 01:16:38.810
Today, before I came on this podcast with you,

1681
01:16:38.810 --> 01:16:41.448
like I woke up, I checked my phone,

1682
01:16:41.448 --> 01:16:43.770
I saw what was happening on Twitter,

1683
01:16:43.770 --> 01:16:45.546
checked my Instagram feed,

1684
01:16:45.546 --> 01:16:49.090
asked Alexa what the weather was gonna be like,

1685
01:16:49.090 --> 01:16:51.350
listened to a Spotify playlist

1686
01:16:52.519 --> 01:16:54.580
that was automatically generated for me.

1687
01:16:54.580 --> 01:16:59.258
Like, I am not immune to these forces either.

1688
01:16:59.258 --> 01:17:04.258
And all of that is kind of making us less individualized,

1689
01:17:04.800 --> 01:17:07.440
and differentiated and making us sort of more homogenous,

1690
01:17:07.440 --> 01:17:09.460
and I think less human.

1691
01:17:09.460 --> 01:17:12.030
And so one of the things that I tried to do

1692
01:17:12.030 --> 01:17:16.050
as part of this book is do this kind of phone detox,

1693
01:17:16.050 --> 01:17:18.228
but it was not really about using my phone less.

1694
01:17:18.228 --> 01:17:20.970
Like, I think we all use our phones a lot.

1695
01:17:20.970 --> 01:17:23.520
I'm not sure, what's your screen time like these days?

1696
01:17:23.520 --> 01:17:27.600
I'm embarrassed to admit, I mean, just so many hours,

1697
01:17:27.600 --> 01:17:29.700
I mean, with the pandemic it's ramped up,

1698
01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:33.070
and it's just embarrassing as somebody who hosts a podcast

1699
01:17:33.070 --> 01:17:36.520
about well-being to then get that alert on Sunday

1700
01:17:36.520 --> 01:17:39.020
that tells you how much you've been on your phone.

1701
01:17:39.933 --> 01:17:42.720
I know, I hate that alert so much for the last year.

1702
01:17:42.720 --> 01:17:45.631
I'm trying to disable it, but you can't I don't think.

1703
01:17:45.631 --> 01:17:47.570
No, it wants to shame you,

1704
01:17:47.570 --> 01:17:50.210
and it wants to tell you how badly you're doing,

1705
01:17:50.210 --> 01:17:52.230
but I actually don't think,

1706
01:17:52.230 --> 01:17:54.660
like, screen time itself is the problem.

1707
01:17:54.660 --> 01:17:55.720
I think the problem is that

1708
01:17:55.720 --> 01:17:57.720
we delegate authority to our phones.

1709
01:17:57.720 --> 01:18:00.830
If you remember, like, the first phone you got,

1710
01:18:00.830 --> 01:18:02.730
it was a tool, right?

1711
01:18:02.730 --> 01:18:05.180
Like, you used it to call people.

1712
01:18:05.180 --> 01:18:07.630
You might have had texting, like, the T9 texting.

1713
01:18:07.630 --> 01:18:08.810
Like, you would use that.

1714
01:18:08.810 --> 01:18:10.530
You would use it as a calculator.

1715
01:18:10.530 --> 01:18:12.286
You might use it to play "Brick Breaker,"

1716
01:18:12.286 --> 01:18:14.890
or "Sneak," or whatever, but it was a tool.

1717
01:18:14.890 --> 01:18:17.950
And then over time they became

1718
01:18:17.950 --> 01:18:19.490
sort of our bosses in this way.

1719
01:18:19.490 --> 01:18:22.209
They tell us what to think about, what to do,

1720
01:18:22.209 --> 01:18:25.960
what to care about, what news stories to read,

1721
01:18:25.960 --> 01:18:28.360
who to communicate with, how to communicate with them.

1722
01:18:28.360 --> 01:18:30.900
They sort of run our lives

1723
01:18:30.900 --> 01:18:32.710
in this way that I think is very harmful.

1724
01:18:32.710 --> 01:18:35.000
And so my phone detox,

1725
01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:37.770
which I actually got a phone coach for,

1726
01:18:37.770 --> 01:18:40.759
who guided me through this 30 day process,

1727
01:18:40.759 --> 01:18:43.950
like going to rehab for phone addiction.

1728
01:18:43.950 --> 01:18:46.963
That might be a futureproof occupation.

1729
01:18:47.910 --> 01:18:51.300
Oh, there are gonna be phone coaches

1730
01:18:51.300 --> 01:18:54.653
in every town in America like this is gonna be.

1731
01:18:54.653 --> 01:18:56.143
I didn't even know that was a thing.

1732
01:18:56.143 --> 01:18:59.260
If I were a brazen capitalist, I would open up,

1733
01:18:59.260 --> 01:19:03.670
like, the world's largest center for phone rehab.

1734
01:19:03.670 --> 01:19:06.210
And I would just have hundreds of thousands of people

1735
01:19:06.210 --> 01:19:09.690
streaming into my rehab centers everyday

1736
01:19:09.690 --> 01:19:12.790
'cause they can't think for themselves anymore.

1737
01:19:12.790 --> 01:19:15.440
So yeah, I think that's part of what I did

1738
01:19:15.440 --> 01:19:18.700
to sort of try to futureproof myself was to say, like,

1739
01:19:18.700 --> 01:19:20.730
it's not just about using your phone less.

1740
01:19:20.730 --> 01:19:22.040
It's not about being distracted.

1741
01:19:22.040 --> 01:19:23.850
It's not about being productive.

1742
01:19:23.850 --> 01:19:26.840
It's literally about, like, figuring out who you are,

1743
01:19:26.840 --> 01:19:30.610
and what you actually value, and separating these categories

1744
01:19:30.610 --> 01:19:33.090
of like identification and identity,

1745
01:19:33.090 --> 01:19:36.300
because I think our phones and the technology in our lives

1746
01:19:36.300 --> 01:19:38.200
want us to blur those things together.

1747
01:19:38.200 --> 01:19:42.320
So I try to spend at least an hour everyday

1748
01:19:42.320 --> 01:19:44.818
just like no phone around me, I call it human hour.

1749
01:19:44.818 --> 01:19:49.818
And I go work in my garden, or I take the dog out,

1750
01:19:49.930 --> 01:19:52.190
or I do something that is just my time

1751
01:19:52.190 --> 01:19:56.496
to kind of remind myself, like, I am not my digital avatar.

1752
01:19:56.496 --> 01:19:59.840
I am not a creature of the internet.

1753
01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:03.240
I actually am a person in the offline world who has desires,

1754
01:20:03.240 --> 01:20:06.298
and hopes, and beliefs, and values.

1755
01:20:06.298 --> 01:20:09.395
And so that's sort of my daily chores to remind myself,

1756
01:20:09.395 --> 01:20:12.550
like, I am not who I show up as on the internet.

1757
01:20:12.550 --> 01:20:13.600
Yeah, it's super powerful.

1758
01:20:13.600 --> 01:20:15.710
I mean, this is something that Cal Newport

1759
01:20:15.710 --> 01:20:17.550
has written extensively about.

1760
01:20:17.550 --> 01:20:19.590
Everybody should read his book, "Digital Minimalism."

1761
01:20:19.590 --> 01:20:22.874
He's got this new book out about a world beyond email.

1762
01:20:22.874 --> 01:20:26.330
And I think the kind of mental leap,

1763
01:20:26.330 --> 01:20:27.928
or what makes it so challenging,

1764
01:20:27.928 --> 01:20:31.640
especially, for somebody like yourself or myself,

1765
01:20:31.640 --> 01:20:36.070
who plies their trade online to some degree

1766
01:20:36.070 --> 01:20:40.080
there's a feeling, or a fear of irrelevance,

1767
01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:42.430
or if you opt out for a minute

1768
01:20:42.430 --> 01:20:45.930
that suddenly you're jeopardizing

1769
01:20:45.930 --> 01:20:49.900
what it is that you do for a living, and decoupling that,

1770
01:20:49.900 --> 01:20:53.387
like, trying to understand that that's actually not true,

1771
01:20:53.387 --> 01:20:58.210
and that it is core to the quality of what you do

1772
01:20:58.210 --> 01:21:02.888
for a living to put distance between yourself and technology

1773
01:21:02.888 --> 01:21:06.536
from time to time, but it's hard.

1774
01:21:06.536 --> 01:21:09.209
It's hard, no, I'm not saying it's easy.

1775
01:21:09.209 --> 01:21:11.890
Look, I struggle with this everyday.

1776
01:21:11.890 --> 01:21:16.890
And my wife will tell you, like, I am not an ideal

1777
01:21:18.280 --> 01:21:20.700
phone free meditator.

1778
01:21:20.700 --> 01:21:22.880
Well, you have the ultimate, like, excuse.

1779
01:21:22.880 --> 01:21:23.970
It's like, this is my beat.

1780
01:21:23.970 --> 01:21:25.480
I have to know what's going on in tech.

1781
01:21:25.480 --> 01:21:26.900
I have to be on this all the time.

1782
01:21:26.900 --> 01:21:28.160
Things are happening quickly.

1783
01:21:28.160 --> 01:21:30.370
This is what I'm writing about.

1784
01:21:30.370 --> 01:21:33.163
Exactly, if I'm up at one a.m. looking at TikToks,

1785
01:21:33.163 --> 01:21:36.270
I mean, that's work, those are billable hours.

1786
01:21:36.270 --> 01:21:39.000
No, but I think that's sort of the story I tell myself,

1787
01:21:39.000 --> 01:21:42.169
but in reality, like, these are very powerful machines.

1788
01:21:42.169 --> 01:21:44.343
Every time you look at your phone,

1789
01:21:44.343 --> 01:21:47.040
you are looking at a device

1790
01:21:47.040 --> 01:21:49.790
on which hundreds of billions of dollars have been spent

1791
01:21:49.790 --> 01:21:52.700
to make you distracted

1792
01:21:52.700 --> 01:21:54.929
from whatever else you might have been thinking about.

1793
01:21:54.929 --> 01:21:58.356
The world's smartest engineers are working on

1794
01:21:58.356 --> 01:22:01.780
how to stop you from looking away from your phone.

1795
01:22:01.780 --> 01:22:03.700
And so I think we tell ourselves

1796
01:22:03.700 --> 01:22:05.450
that we need to be plugged in,

1797
01:22:05.450 --> 01:22:07.607
but, actually, I think there's something happening right now

1798
01:22:07.607 --> 01:22:10.715
where, like, I am in awe of people

1799
01:22:10.715 --> 01:22:15.000
who are not slaves to their technology.

1800
01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:17.360
I mean, sometimes, even when you read a book,

1801
01:22:17.360 --> 01:22:20.660
you can just tell like, oh, this person is not on Twitter.

1802
01:22:20.660 --> 01:22:22.260
It's almost transgressive

1803
01:22:22.260 --> 01:22:24.163
when you see somebody reading a book.

1804
01:22:25.040 --> 01:22:29.530
Yeah, and it's like they're more evolved than I am.

1805
01:22:29.530 --> 01:22:32.330
Like, they are not in the same.

1806
01:22:32.330 --> 01:22:36.050
Like, they are eating artisanal slow food.

1807
01:22:36.050 --> 01:22:38.880
And I am over here, like, chowing down on KFC,

1808
01:22:38.880 --> 01:22:40.390
and like, we are not the same.

1809
01:22:40.390 --> 01:22:43.555
And so I think in the present and in the future

1810
01:22:43.555 --> 01:22:46.580
there will be a real value

1811
01:22:46.580 --> 01:22:49.750
to being able to separate yourself from your phone,

1812
01:22:49.750 --> 01:22:51.737
and your feeds and your technology,

1813
01:22:51.737 --> 01:22:54.910
because that's how we get better.

1814
01:22:54.910 --> 01:22:58.450
I mean, everything in life that makes us better,

1815
01:22:58.450 --> 01:23:01.090
very little of it happens through the phone.

1816
01:23:01.090 --> 01:23:04.440
It happens through taking on personal challenges,

1817
01:23:04.440 --> 01:23:07.230
through struggling, through raising families,

1818
01:23:07.230 --> 01:23:08.890
through building communities.

1819
01:23:08.890 --> 01:23:10.600
Like that's the stuff that is really hard,

1820
01:23:10.600 --> 01:23:11.800
and really rewarding.

1821
01:23:11.800 --> 01:23:15.970
Until we graduate to the ultimate virtual world

1822
01:23:15.970 --> 01:23:18.330
where we can just upload our consciousness

1823
01:23:18.330 --> 01:23:20.070
into an alternate reality,

1824
01:23:20.070 --> 01:23:22.653
and create the utopia of our dreams.

1825
01:23:23.780 --> 01:23:27.104
Exactly, I want the "WALL-E" chairs, like I want,

1826
01:23:27.104 --> 01:23:31.370
I just want to be driven around by a robot,

1827
01:23:31.370 --> 01:23:33.806
and drink my Big Gulp some days.

1828
01:23:33.806 --> 01:23:36.793
To your law school class at Walmart.

1829
01:23:38.030 --> 01:23:39.373
Exactly, exactly.

1830
01:23:41.300 --> 01:23:45.330
If you were giving counsel to a young person now

1831
01:23:45.330 --> 01:23:47.560
who's listening to this who's grappling with like,

1832
01:23:47.560 --> 01:23:48.810
well, what should I pursue,

1833
01:23:48.810 --> 01:23:52.706
or what would be the best way to set myself up

1834
01:23:52.706 --> 01:23:55.270
to be futureproof?

1835
01:23:55.270 --> 01:23:57.590
What are some of the things other than just the general,

1836
01:23:57.590 --> 01:23:59.709
like, double down on the soft skills,

1837
01:23:59.709 --> 01:24:02.730
like, where is your head in terms of,

1838
01:24:02.730 --> 01:24:04.670
like, where you would direct that person?

1839
01:24:04.670 --> 01:24:06.747
Well, there's a chapter in the book called

1840
01:24:06.747 --> 01:24:08.930
"Learn machine age humanities."

1841
01:24:08.930 --> 01:24:12.080
And I don't think that just studying classics,

1842
01:24:12.080 --> 01:24:15.940
or philosophy, or art history is enough.

1843
01:24:15.940 --> 01:24:17.709
Like, I don't think that's gonna prepare people,

1844
01:24:17.709 --> 01:24:19.660
but I do think there are these skills

1845
01:24:19.660 --> 01:24:22.820
that we can teach people that will help them,

1846
01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:24.210
no matter what they choose to study.

1847
01:24:24.210 --> 01:24:27.186
So one of them is this idea of guarding our attention.

1848
01:24:27.186 --> 01:24:30.040
I think that's a really underrated skill.

1849
01:24:30.040 --> 01:24:32.187
People who are able to kind of control

1850
01:24:32.187 --> 01:24:35.350
what they pay attention to, what they think about.

1851
01:24:35.350 --> 01:24:36.850
Whether that's meditation,

1852
01:24:36.850 --> 01:24:39.380
whether it's getting off their phones,

1853
01:24:39.380 --> 01:24:40.410
whether it's reading books,

1854
01:24:40.410 --> 01:24:41.987
I think that's a really important skill.

1855
01:24:41.987 --> 01:24:45.319
I think there are other skills that we can teach people,

1856
01:24:45.319 --> 01:24:48.338
like the value of rest.

1857
01:24:48.338 --> 01:24:51.170
I mean, there's a really counterintuitive thing

1858
01:24:51.170 --> 01:24:52.700
that I sort of came across,

1859
01:24:52.700 --> 01:24:54.950
which is that a lot of the people I knew

1860
01:24:54.950 --> 01:24:59.950
who were the highest ranking people in AI and engineering,

1861
01:25:00.440 --> 01:25:04.900
hyper, well-respected, thoughtful people

1862
01:25:04.900 --> 01:25:08.367
were actually not working, like, 17 hour days.

1863
01:25:08.367 --> 01:25:10.310
They were taking time off.

1864
01:25:10.310 --> 01:25:13.247
They were going home to their families.

1865
01:25:13.247 --> 01:25:16.030
One of them took naps during the day.

1866
01:25:16.030 --> 01:25:18.420
I mean, these are like the kinds of things

1867
01:25:18.420 --> 01:25:21.190
that we would think, oh, that person's a slacker.

1868
01:25:21.190 --> 01:25:22.060
They're not very productive,

1869
01:25:22.060 --> 01:25:24.230
but, actually, that's what allows them to do

1870
01:25:24.230 --> 01:25:26.680
the kinds of creative and thoughtful human work

1871
01:25:26.680 --> 01:25:28.760
that machines can't replicate.

1872
01:25:28.760 --> 01:25:31.690
Yeah, an amazing example of that would be

1873
01:25:31.690 --> 01:25:32.970
Yuval Noah Harari,

1874
01:25:32.970 --> 01:25:36.618
who goes on these extended silent meditation retreats.

1875
01:25:36.618 --> 01:25:39.060
And he credits those experiences,

1876
01:25:39.060 --> 01:25:41.040
in addition to his daily,

1877
01:25:41.040 --> 01:25:43.550
however many hours he does it every single day,

1878
01:25:43.550 --> 01:25:46.140
with his ability to have that kind of

1879
01:25:46.140 --> 01:25:49.930
objective perspective on really big issues,

1880
01:25:49.930 --> 01:25:53.802
and the clarity that he can instill his writing with.

1881
01:25:53.802 --> 01:25:58.460
Yeah, and I'm not as good at meditating as that.

1882
01:25:58.460 --> 01:25:59.840
Very few are.

1883
01:25:59.840 --> 01:26:04.840
I top out at 10 or 12 minutes on my Calm app, but I try,

1884
01:26:05.580 --> 01:26:07.840
and I think that's a really important skill

1885
01:26:07.840 --> 01:26:09.810
for people to develop.

1886
01:26:09.810 --> 01:26:12.930
And I also think that we're starting to see

1887
01:26:12.930 --> 01:26:14.970
that there are classes and curriculums

1888
01:26:14.970 --> 01:26:16.883
that are being sort of adapted slowly,

1889
01:26:16.883 --> 01:26:20.960
and mostly at sort of high-end elite schools,

1890
01:26:20.960 --> 01:26:23.933
but you're starting to see things like there's a course,

1891
01:26:23.933 --> 01:26:25.291
I think at Stanford.

1892
01:26:25.291 --> 01:26:27.620
I don't know what its actual title is,

1893
01:26:27.620 --> 01:26:31.730
but people refer to it as empathy for engineers.

1894
01:26:31.730 --> 01:26:34.170
So they basically take people who are computer scientists,

1895
01:26:34.170 --> 01:26:36.000
and teach them about people,

1896
01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:38.063
teach them how to be emotionally intelligent,

1897
01:26:38.063 --> 01:26:40.770
how to read people's emotions, how to talk to people.

1898
01:26:40.770 --> 01:26:44.330
It's sort of like remedial people skills

1899
01:26:44.330 --> 01:26:48.717
for super high IQ engineers.

1900
01:26:48.717 --> 01:26:51.550
And you're starting to see that in more disciplines,

1901
01:26:51.550 --> 01:26:54.340
there are medical school classes now

1902
01:26:54.340 --> 01:26:57.420
where they don't even talk about medicine.

1903
01:26:57.420 --> 01:26:59.130
It's about how to talk to patients.

1904
01:26:59.130 --> 01:27:02.110
It's about how to communicate with them empathetically,

1905
01:27:02.110 --> 01:27:04.560
and to solve their problems and to hear them out.

1906
01:27:05.624 --> 01:27:08.100
There's a venture capitalist, Frank Chen,

1907
01:27:08.100 --> 01:27:10.182
who invests in a lot of AI startups,

1908
01:27:10.182 --> 01:27:14.753
and the book that he recommends to people who work in AI

1909
01:27:14.753 --> 01:27:15.997
is this book called

1910
01:27:15.997 --> 01:27:18.314
"Everything I Need To Know I Learned in Kindergarten."

1911
01:27:18.314 --> 01:27:23.124
And it's written decades ago by this by minister.

1912
01:27:23.124 --> 01:27:24.760
And it's all about kind of like

1913
01:27:24.760 --> 01:27:27.920
the basic skills of human existence,

1914
01:27:27.920 --> 01:27:32.340
sharing, things that we learn in kindergarten,

1915
01:27:32.340 --> 01:27:33.446
and then kind of forget.

1916
01:27:33.446 --> 01:27:37.380
And so his big thing is those are the skills of the future.

1917
01:27:37.380 --> 01:27:38.550
And I agree.

1918
01:27:38.550 --> 01:27:41.540
I think that we all could use a refresher course

1919
01:27:41.540 --> 01:27:42.995
on the things that we learn in kindergarten.

1920
01:27:42.995 --> 01:27:45.140
Yeah, that's powerful.

1921
01:27:45.140 --> 01:27:47.840
I mean, in the most general sense, I suppose,

1922
01:27:47.840 --> 01:27:52.570
it's about learning how to live your life

1923
01:27:55.530 --> 01:27:59.480
non-reactively and more consciously, right?

1924
01:27:59.480 --> 01:28:02.733
To the extent that you can develop an objective sense,

1925
01:28:04.519 --> 01:28:07.520
an objectivity around just how much

1926
01:28:07.520 --> 01:28:11.950
you're reacting to technology and devices,

1927
01:28:11.950 --> 01:28:15.620
and develop the skillsets to kind of transcend that

1928
01:28:15.620 --> 01:28:18.980
so that you can have the interior life,

1929
01:28:18.980 --> 01:28:22.470
and bring that into not just your work,

1930
01:28:22.470 --> 01:28:26.210
but your families and your communities,

1931
01:28:26.210 --> 01:28:29.040
that seems to be the antidote.

1932
01:28:29.040 --> 01:28:31.350
Yeah, and I think that's gonna be a big part

1933
01:28:31.350 --> 01:28:33.100
of how we get out of this.

1934
01:28:33.100 --> 01:28:35.640
I mean, especially coming out of the pandemic,

1935
01:28:35.640 --> 01:28:36.473
I think we're gonna need

1936
01:28:36.473 --> 01:28:39.870
a lot of really intentional work on ourselves

1937
01:28:39.870 --> 01:28:43.680
because for the last year we've been experiencing the world

1938
01:28:43.680 --> 01:28:45.640
primarily through our screens.

1939
01:28:45.640 --> 01:28:49.360
We've sort of conflated who we are on the internet

1940
01:28:49.360 --> 01:28:51.090
with who we are offline.

1941
01:28:51.090 --> 01:28:53.590
And I think we have a lot of rebuilding to do there

1942
01:28:53.590 --> 01:28:55.490
of getting back out in the community,

1943
01:28:55.490 --> 01:28:57.446
getting back face-to-face with people

1944
01:28:57.446 --> 01:28:59.470
when it's safe to do so,

1945
01:28:59.470 --> 01:29:02.210
like figuring out what we actually care about,

1946
01:29:02.210 --> 01:29:04.815
and value, and who we are because I think,

1947
01:29:04.815 --> 01:29:08.890
and Yuval Noah Harari made this point in one of his books,

1948
01:29:08.890 --> 01:29:12.210
like, if the algorithms

1949
01:29:12.210 --> 01:29:15.090
know you better than you know yourself,

1950
01:29:15.090 --> 01:29:18.060
then the power will transfer to them.

1951
01:29:18.060 --> 01:29:20.430
Like, you will no longer be in the driver's seat

1952
01:29:20.430 --> 01:29:23.730
of your own life, these algorithms and AIs will,

1953
01:29:23.730 --> 01:29:25.500
and that's not a good place to be.

1954
01:29:25.500 --> 01:29:26.540
And I think that's something

1955
01:29:26.540 --> 01:29:28.200
that I am conscious of everyday,

1956
01:29:28.200 --> 01:29:30.723
and trying to sort of swim in the other direction from.

1957
01:29:30.723 --> 01:29:35.683
Well, I think that's a good place to kind of round it out.

1958
01:29:37.210 --> 01:29:38.717
I love the book.

1959
01:29:38.717 --> 01:29:40.120
This was so fun.

1960
01:29:40.120 --> 01:29:40.980
This is cool, man.

